4400:
** προχειρ-ίζω**,
**make** πρόχειρος, **deliver up**, -κεχειρικέναι τινὰ ἐπὶ τὸ (perhaps τῷ) τὴν τιμωρίαν δοῦναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 3.14}}:—_passive_, mostly in _participle_, **taken in hand, undertaken**, τὸν προκεχειρις μένον λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 643a; προκεχειρισμένα καὶ ἑτοῖμα ἀγαθά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.33.}}
**2** **produce**, ὡς ἴδια αὐτοὶ ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτῶν π. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 11.}}
**II** more frequently in _middle_ προχειρίζομαι, _future_ **-χειριοῦμαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 729}}:—**make ready for oneself, mobilize**, προχειριοῦμαι κἀξετάσω τὴν οὐσίαν Ar.[same place]; δύναμιν, στρατόπεδα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.19, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.16.2, 3.107.10; ἐσθῆτα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 14; τὴν μαλάχην [prev. author] “VH” 2.46}}; τὰς ῥήσεις, τοὺς λογισμούς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.396c,813d; βιβλίον 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.555.}}
**II.2** **choose, select**, δημαγωγούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.122; τοὺς τὴν πίστιν.. τηρήσοντας “OGI” 339.46 (Sestos, 2nd c.BC)}}; γραμματέα κοινὸν ἐκ περιόδου {{LXX+4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.43.1, cf. LXX.Josh.3.12, +others; τινὰ ἐπί τι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.13; ἐπί τινι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 58; πρός τι τοὺς ἐπιτηδείους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.44.4}}; **appoint**, τινὰ δικτάτορα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 54.1, compare “IG” 22.1110.14}}:—_passive_, -ισθεὶς.. ἀγωνοθέτης {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 268.4 (Nacrasa, 3rd c.BC),cf. LXX.Dan.3.22, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.106.2, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.27, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 58.20; ὑπὸ τοῦ βασιλέως 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.3.4, cf. Wilcken “Chr.” 12.13 (1st c.BC)}}; -ισθεὶς θεωρός {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 341 (a). 25 (3rd c.BC), compare 42.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὑπὸ τῶν πολιτῶν ἐπὶ τὴν στρατηγίαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.66, compare “BMus.Inscription” 1044 (from Attaleia), “PTeb.” 27.22 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**II.2.b** **allot, assign**, in _passive_, διὰ τὸ -κεχειρίσθαι αὐτῷ τὴν γῆν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 132.3 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ Ποπλίῳ -κεχειρισμένα στρατόπεδα {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.40.14; τὸν -κεχειρισμένον ὑμῖν Χριστὸν Ἰησοῦν NT.Act.3.20.}}
**II.3** **prefer**, οἱ Δημοσθένην -χειρισάμενοι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Dinarcho” 8.}}
**II.4** with _infinitive_, **determine** to do, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.40.2}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **decide**, τῆς πόλεως -κεχειρισμένης τὸν ἀγῶνα.. στεφανίτην εἶναι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 457.14 (Thespiae, 3rd c.BC).}}
**II.5** **discuss** or **examine**, τὰς ἄλλας κατηγορίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 10b19; τὰς πάντων δόξας [prev. author] “Top.” 105a35; also π. περί τινος [prev. author] “Cael.” 271b25, compare “Ph.” 200b23}}; περὶ ἕκαστον γένος (variant{ἑκάστου γένους}) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 378b6.}}
**II.6** ἐπὶ πραδείγματος π. **propose** by way of example, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 953a33}}:— _passive_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cat.” 2a36; ὁ περὶ τῶν -χειρισθέντων λόγος 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.14.}}

4401:
** προχειροτον-έω**,
**choose, elect before**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 765b (_passive_)}}; μάρτυρες -κεχειροτονημένοι ὑπὸ θεοῦ {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Act.10.41; ἐς ὑπατείαν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 50.4 (_passive_).}}
**2** **take a preliminary** or **previous vote**, περὶ ἱερῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.23, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.11.}}

4403:
** πρύμν-ᾰ**,
_accusative_ **-ᾰν**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.50, +others, “PCair.Zen.” 54.8 (3rd c.BC)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **πρύμνη** (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 482, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 399 (_anapaest meter_)}}; both forms in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.114 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus): ἡ:}} —properly _feminine_ of πρυμνός (i.e. ναῦς), **stern, poop**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus mostly πρύμνη νηῦς in full, νηῒ πάρα πρύμνῃ, νηὸς ἄπο πρύμνης, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.383, +others, 15.435; ἐπὶ πρύμνῃ.. νηΐ 11.600}}; νηῒ ἐνὶ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.417: _plural_, νηυσὶν ἔπι πρύμνῃσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.403; ἐπὶ π. νέεσσι 13.333; ἐπὶ πρύμνῃσιν (ναῦφι going before) 8.475}}; but also τῆς (i.e. νηός) πρύμνη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.84, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a, 58c}}; π. alone, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.409, +others:—Phrases: πρύμνην ἀνακρούεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.84}}; also _metaphorically_ of a man, Ar.[same place]; χωρεῖν πρύμναν **retire, draw back**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1120}}; ἐπείγει κατὰ πρύμναν, of a fair wind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1451 (_anapaest meter_); κατὰ π. ἵσταται τὸ πνεῦμα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97}}; ἄγειν ἑαυτὴν ἐκ πρύμνης, _metaphorically_ of the soul, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 400.—Ships were generally drawn up on land by the stern, [νῆας] πεδίονδε εἴρυσαν, αὐτὰρ τεῖχος ἐπὶ πρύμνῃσιν ἔδειμαν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.32; κατὰ πρύμνας καὶ ἀμφ᾽ ἅλα ἔλσαι Ἀχαιούς 1.409; πρύμνας λῦσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 539.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, π. πόλεος **the Acropolis**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 345}}; also of **the vessel** of the State, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 2, 760 (Lyric poetry)}}; so ἐκ πρύμνης φρενός, probably in {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 989.}}
**II** **bottom, foot**, π. Ὄσσας uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 445 (Lyric poetry).}}

4404:
** πρωΐ** [ῐ],
_Attic dialect_ **πρῴ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(Hdn.Gr. 1.494, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 132, etc.), though codices commonly give πρῶϊ, πρωΐ, or πρῷ}}: _adverb_: (πρό):— **early in the day, at morn**, opposed to ὀψέ (_accusative_ to {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 9}}, **the forenoon, between** ἀνατολή **and** μεσημβρία), πρωῒ (variant{πρῶϊ}) ὑπηοῖοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.530, +others: with _genitive_, πρωῒ ἔτι τῆς ἡμέρης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.101; ἑκάστης ἡμέρας τὸ πρῴ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.30, compare “PSI” 3.402.10 (3rd c.BC): also πρῲ πάνυ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 104}}; πρῲ τῇ ὑστεραίᾳ **early** next morning, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.16; τὸ πρωῒ LXX.Gen.19.27; ἅμα πρωΐ NT.Matt.20.1; ἀπὸ πρωῒ ἕως ἑσπέρας NT.Act.28.23.}}
**2** generally, **betimes, early**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 461, “fragment” 204, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 132, etc.: with _genitive_, πρωῒ τοῦ ἦρος, τοῦ θέρεος, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.1, 2.}}
**3** ={πρὸ καιροῦ} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.106 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus)}}, **too soon, too early**, πρῴ γε στενάζεις (variant{πρό}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 696; δέδοικα γὰρ μὴ πρῲ λέγοις ἄν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 631; πρῲ ἐσβαλόντες, καὶ τοῦ σίτου ἔτι χλωροῦ ὄντος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 135c.— _comparative_ and _superlative_ πρωΐτερον (or πρῴτερον), πρωΐτατα (or πρῴτατα) are found in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.19, 39, 8.101, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Indica” 26.4, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 47 (23). 35, 51(27).51, variant in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.1.6, 2.3.2, 6.8.13, +others}}; but usually πρωϊαίτερον (or πρῳαίτερον), πρωϊαίτατα (or πρῳαίτατα), Hp. [prev. cited] (variant), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59d, 59e, “Tht.” 150e, “Prt.” 326c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.8.9, etc.}}

4405:
Included with: ** πρώϊ-ος**,
_Attic dialect_ **πρῷος**, α, ον: (πρωΐ, πρῴ):— **early**,
**I** **early in the day, at early morn**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.470 (_neuter_ πρώϊον as}} _adverb_ = {πρωΐ}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|); π. ἐμπολέα “Anthologia Graeca” 6.304 (Phanias Epigrammaticus); π. ῥόδον 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 27; also περὶ δείλην πρωΐην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.6; δείλης πρωΐας 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 210.}}
**I.2** _substantive_ **πρωΐα**, ἡ, **early morning**, ἅμα τῇ π.{{NT|Aristeas 304; ἦν δὲ πρωΐα NT.John.18.28; πρωΐας γενομένης NT.Matt.27.1: _genitive_ πρωΐας as}} _adverb_ = {πρῴ}{{Refs|[prev. work] 21.18}}: with Preps., καθ᾽ ἑκάστην πρωΐαν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 7.8.1; ἀπὸ πρωΐας ἄχρις ἡλίου δύσεως “IG” 4.597.16 (from Argos), compare “PLond.” 3.1177.66 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **early in** **the year**, πρώϊος [ὁ στρατὸς] συνελέγετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.130}}; τῶν καρπίμων ἅττα μή 'στι π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 264; σικύων πρῴων [prev. author] “Pax” 1001, compare 1164 (Lyric poetry), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.11.1}}; π. χειμών an **early** winter, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sign.” 40}}; τὸν πρώϊον (or πρῷον) σῖτον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 155.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; διὰ τὸ τὰ μὲν πρώϊα, τὰ δ᾽ ὄψια προΐεσθαι (i.e. ᾠά) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 543a9}}; π. τόπος an **early** place, i.e. producing early fruits, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.2.9}}: _comparative_ πρωΐτερος {{Refs|[prev. author] “CP” 5.6.5}} codices **πρωϊ-ότης**, ητος, ἡ, **earliness**, of fruits, {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.11.9.}}

4406:
** πρώϊμος**, ον,
_Ionic dialect_ **πρόϊμος** conjecture in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 482 (“Hermes” 24.453)}}:— **early**, of fruits, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 17.4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 924b5, “OGI” 56.68 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; of winter, {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.172}}; ὥρη Call.[same place]; also, **born early**, ἄρνες {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 771.10 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **precocious**, π. πονηρία {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “fragment” 56}}. _adverb_ _comparative_ **-ώτερον**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 27.25 (2nd c.BC).}}

4408:
** πρῷρ-α** [ᾰ
in _nominative_ and _accusative_ _singular_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 716, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 482, “fragment” 726, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1563, 1582, “Or.” 362, “IT” 1134 (Lyric poetry)}}; _nominative_ πρῷρ᾽ with elision in {{Refs|“IG” 2.2836}}; _accusative_ πρῴρᾱν is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.372}}; πρῴρην is found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194, 7.180]}}, ἡ, **forepart of a ship, prow**, εἰς ἴκρια νηὸς πρῴρης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.230 (here probably adjectival with νηός),compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} [same places], etc.; πνεῦμα τοὐκ πρῴρας a contrary wind, opposed to κατὰ πρύμναν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 639.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, πρῷρα βιότου **the prow** of life's vessel, i.e. early youth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 103 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ὦ πρῷρα λοιβῆς Ἑστία **thou who art first entitled to** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 726}}; πάροιθεν πρῴρας.. καρδίας before my heart's **prow**, in front of my heart, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 390 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **end** of a vinebranch, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.1.3 (conjecture in “CP” 3.14.7). (Written πρώρρα in 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.6.1, +others, but πρωιρ- in “PSI” 4.382.2 (3rd c.BC)}}, compare πρῳρατικό; κυανοπρωΐρους [??] is cited by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 692.32 from 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, and κυανοπρώϊραν from 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 241: hence πρῷρα is probably _contraction_ from a word of the form ¯ ¯ ?~X, but whether from *πρώειρα, as inferred by 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 2.410}}, is doubtful; perhaps from *πρώαιρα, compare νείαιρ; -πρωῑρ- in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus may have arisen by 'distraction' of the contracted form.)}}

4409:
** πρωτεύω**,
(πρῶτος) **to be the first, hold first place**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.41 (assuming variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 692d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1124b23}}; οἱ πρωτεύοντες **the primates** or **chief men** in a city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.7.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.68}}; **hold position of** πρῶτος (which see), {{Refs|“MAMA” 4.151 (from Apollonia)}}; π. τοῦ ἔθνους {{Refs|“OGI” 563.6 (from Cadyanda).}}
**II** with a modal word added, **to be first in** a thing, καρτερίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 10.1; βδελυρίᾳ 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.192; γένει 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.21; ἐν ἕδρᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.5; περὶ κακίαν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.159; φιλίᾳ π. παρὰ τῷ Κύρῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.28, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.60}}; ἐν τούτοις (i.e. ἐπιτηδεύμασιν) {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.48.}}
**II.2** with _genitive_ _person_, **to be first of** or **among**, Ἑλλήνων {{Refs|[prev. work]6}}; τῶν ῥητόρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.171, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.3; π. Ἑλλάδος εἰς ἀρετήν “epigram Gr.” 489: also π. ἐν τοῖς Ἕλλησι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.24; ἐν τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.297.}}
**III** ={προτερέω}, **excel**, π. τῆς Ἀρτέμιδος ταῖς κυνηγεσίαις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.81.}}

4414:
** πρωτο-στάτης** [ᾰ], ου, ὁ,
(ἵστημι) **one who stands first**, especially **the first man on the right** of a line, **right-hand man**, ὁ π. τοῦ δεξιοῦ κέρως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.71}}; but also οἱ π. **the front-rank men**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.57, 6.3.24, “Lac.” 11.5, etc.}}; either sense possible in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.4 H.}}
**2** ={λοχαγός}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 5.1, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 5.6.}}
**3** **man in the uneven rows in a** λόχος, opposed to ἐπιστάτης, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.3, etc.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **chief** or **leader** of a party, {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Act.24.5; π. τοῦ χοροῦ τῶν μαθητῶν 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 26.1}}; π. τοῦ θητικοῦ καὶ οἰκετικοῦ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Men.Prot. p.8 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}

4416:
** πρωτοτόκ-ος**
(_paroxytone_), _Doric dialect_ **πρᾱτοτόκος**, ον, **bearing** or **having borne her first-born**, μήτηρ π., of a heifer, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.5; αἴξ 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.27}}; ὗς, ταὧς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 546a12, 564a30; κύων 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.70.6}}; of women, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 151c, 161a; νύμφη 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Lithica” 193.}}
**II** _proparoxytone_ πρωτότοκος, ον, _passive_,**first-born**, {{LXX+NT+4th c.AD+|LXX.Gen.22.21, +others, NT.Luke.2.7, “PLips.” 28.15 (4th c.AD), 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 3.9; τὰ π. τῶν προβάτων LXX.Gen.4.4, compare “PMag. Osl.” 1.312}}; π. ἐγὼ ἢ σύ {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.19.43.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, π. πάσης κτίσεως {{NT|NT.Col.1.15}}; of Homer, opposed to Nicander, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.213.}}

4417:
** πταίω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122, etc.}}: _future_ πταίσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.20}}: _aorist_ ἔπταισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.101, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἔπταικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 675, 3rd c.BC: Bato Comicus 1, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.48.4, (προσ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.82:}} — _passive_, see #1:
**I** _transitive_, **cause to stumble** or **fall**, σύνθεσιν ποτὶ ψεύδει {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 205, cf. LXX.1Ki.4.3}}:—_passive_, **to be missed**, of things, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.15}}; τὰ πταισθέντα **failures, errors**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 7}}; ἃ ἐπταίσθη his **failures**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Dion.Brut.” 3.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **stumble, trip, fall**, π. πρός τινι **stumble against, fall over**, π., ὥσπερ πρὸς ἕρματι, πρὸς τῇ πόλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553b, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 926, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.26}}; πρὸς τὰς πέτρας conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.2.3; proverbial, μὴ δὶς πρὸς τὸν αὐτὸν λίθον πταίειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.11.5}}; also π. περί τινι, μὴ περὶ Μαρδονίῳ πταίσῃ ἡ Ἑλλάς lest Hellas **should get a fall** over him, i.e. be defeated by him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.101.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **make a false step** or **mistake**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.20, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 672, etc.}}; ἐὰν πταίωσί τι when **they make a blunder**, of medical men, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 75.5}}; οὐκ ἐλάττω, ἐλάχιστα, τὰ πλείω π., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122, 4.18, 6.33; ἔν τισι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.286; λογισμοῖς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 380}}; τῇ μάχῃ, τοῖς ὅλοις, τοῖς πράγμασι, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.14.13, 3.48.4, 1.10.1, etc.; ἀψευδὴς ὢν καὶ μὴ π. τῇ διανοίᾳ περὶ τὰ ὄντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 160d; also π. ὑπ᾽ ἀνάγκας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 215 (Lyric poetry); ὑπό τινος π. τῇ πατρίδι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.93.2; ἐκ τύχης [prev. author] 2.7.3.}}
**II.3** π. τῆς ἐλπίδος **to be baulked of.. **, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.5.1.}}
**II.4** ἡ γλῶττα π. **stutters**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 875b19.}}

4418:
** πτέρνη**
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 3, “Epid.” 5.48, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.)}}, later **πτέρνᾰ** {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|(3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 500, LXX.Gen.3.15, etc.)}}, ἡ, **heel**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.397, Hp. [prev. cited], 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 494b7}}; **under part of the heel**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 209 (_plural_)}}; **heel-bone**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.776, +others: proverbial, εἴπερ τὸν ἐγκέφαλον.. μὴ ἐν ταῖς π. φορεῖτε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.45.}}
**2** **hoof**, {{LXX|LXX.Judg.5.22.}}
**3** **heel** of a shoe, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.21, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.69B.}}
**4** **footstep**, {{LXX|LXX.Cant.1.8.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **foot** or **lower part** of anything, πύργων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 442; τῆς μηχανῆς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.6.2}}; of a mast, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Asclepiades Myrleanus Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.474f}} (but, **waist** of a ship, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Stereom.” 2.52).}}
**II.2** **butt-end** of the ἀγκών of a torsion-engine, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 59.30, 66.2}}; of a surgical machine, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.4.9, +others}}
**III** **ham** (mock Epicism formed from Latin _perna_), {{Refs|Batrachomyomachia 37}}; _falsa lectio_ for{πέρνα} in {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 15.15, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 7.17.74 (πέρνα correctly in 4.32).}}

4419:
** πτερῠγ-ιον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of πτέρυξ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 615b30}}.
**II** **anything like a wing.**
**II.1** in _plural_, **fins** of fish, {{Refs|[prev. work] 489b24, 504b30, “IA” 714a11}}; of the **tail-flaps** of a lobster, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 490a3, compare 525b27, “PA” 684a13, “GA” 720b12}}; of certain **sea-slugs**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 532b22, 24}}; **fins** of the sepia and other cuttle-fish, πτερύγι᾽.. σηπίας ὠπτημένα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Sotades Comicus 1.16, compare 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 187.3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 524a31, “PA” 685b16.}}
**II.2** **horns** of the horned owl, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 597b22.}}
**II.3** **end** or **tip** of the rudder, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.90}}; of a pole, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.4** in a building, **turret** or **battlement**, or (as others) **pointed roof, peak,** {{NT|NT.Luke.4.9; compare πτέρυξ 11.7.}}
**II.5** **flap, fold** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|compare (πτέρυξ 11.4), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 802a39, LXX.Num. 15.38, LXX.Ruth.3.9, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.62}}; **flap** of a cuirass, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 31.8; π. κρανῶν “IG” 22.1424a.399 (_plural_).}}
**II.6** in the body, **part of the shoulderblade**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.177}}; of the ear, **parts joining the temples**,{{Refs 5th c.AD+|[prev. work] 85, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; of the nose, **parts joining the cheeks**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.80, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.71, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 11.12.}}
**II.7** in Medicine texts, disease of the eye when a **membrane grows over it from the inner corner**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.20, 1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus 7.7.4, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.108, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.732.}}
**II.8** **fleshy excrescence** on the nails, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Celsus Medicus 6.19.1, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.110, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.85.}}
**II.9** _plural_,= του ={πνεύμονος τοῦ λοβοῦ τὰ ἄκρα}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II.10** _plural_, **flanges** holding the projector of a torsion-engine, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 54.23; on a κέστρος 11, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 27.11.4.}}

4420:
** πτέρυξ**
(πτερύξ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus cited in Hdn.Gr. 1.45)}}, ῠγος, ἡ: _Epic dialect_ _dative_ _plural_ πτερύγεσσι: (πτερόν):—**wing** of a bird, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.316; mostly _plural_, [prev. work] 462, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.149, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 134, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 52 (_anapaest meter_), etc.}}; λευκὴ πτεροῖσι, πλὴν.. ἄκρων τῶν πτερύγων white in its plumage, save.. the tips of the **wings**, of the ibis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.76}}; of Eros and Nike, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 11.8: _metaphorically_, κινοῦντα πτέρυγας ἤδη}} 'trying your **wings**', {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 155.2.}}
**2** **winged creature, bird,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 6.12 (4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator).}}
**3** **flight, augury, omen**, ποίων (i.e. ὀρνίχων) οὐκ ἀγαθαὶ πτέρυγες {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Lavacrum Palladis” 124.}}
**II** **anything like a wing**,
**II.1** in _plural_,= πτερύγια, **fins** of fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 505b21, “Mir.” 835b10, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.24}}; **flippers** of seals, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 697b5}}; of dolphins and whales, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 537b3}}; of the tortoise, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 559.}}
**II.2** _plural_, **feathery foliage**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.9.6.}}
**II.2.b** ={ἄσπληνος}{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.134.}}
**II.3** **blade** of the steering-paddle, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1607.74}}: hence, **rudder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1083}}; ἑξήρετμοι π., of oars, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 337.2 (from Cyzicus).}}
**II.4** **flap** of a cuirass, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.15 (variant), compare “Eq.” 12.4, 6}}; of the Doric dialect χιτών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 325, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 187, “Comica Adespota” 17.1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Lyc.Num.” 3, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.62.}}
**II.5** **broad edge** of a knife or hunting-spear, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 16, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.21}}; **beak** of the sword-fish (variant{ῥύγχος}), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.40.}}
**II.6** **lobe** of the lungs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 394.}}
**II.7** **point** of a building, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.121.}}
**II.8** **front frame** of a torsion-engine, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 101.8.}}
**II.9** **shoulder-blade**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 30.}}
**II.10** in _plural_, title of poem whose lines form a pattern like wings, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 15.24 (3rd c.BC: Simmias).}}
**II.11** _plural_, **sails,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Comica Adespota 9 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 20.9.}}
**III** **anything that covers** or **protects** like wings, π. πέπλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1143}}; κολπώδη πτέρυγ᾽ Εὐβοίας, i.e. Aulis, {{Refs|[prev. author] “IA” 120 (Lyric poetry); νεοσσὸς ὡσεὶ πτέρυγας εἰσπίτνων ἐμάς [prev. author] “Tr.” 751}}; of a mountain, Λιβάνου πτέρυγες {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Musaeus Epicus 48.}}
**III.2** **fence, wall**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 291.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, πτέρυγες γόων the **wings**, i.e. the **flight** or **flow**, of grief, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 242 (Lyric poetry); π. Πιερίδων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.64.}}

4421:
** πτηνός**, ή, όν,
_Doric dialect_ **πτανός**, ά, όν; also ός, όν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320e}}: (πτῆναι, πέτομαι):— **flying, winged**, Διὸς π. κύων, i.e. eagle, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1022, compare “Ag.” 136 (Lyric poetry); π. ὄφις [prev. author] “Eu.” 181}}; ὄρνις, οἰωνός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 955, “Ant.” 1082}}; Ἔρως, ἵπποι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1275 (Lyric poetry), “IT” 193 (Lyric poetry); ἅρμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 246e}}; also of arrows, π. ἰοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 166 (_anapaest meter_); βέλη 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 179}}; π. φυγή, of birds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320e.}}
**2** τὰ π. **winged creatures, birds**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 591 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 168 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 504 (Lyric poetry), etc.; πτηνὸν ὀρνίθων γένος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1707; πτηνῶν γένη [prev. author] “Th.” 46}}; opposed to τὰ πεζά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 207a}}; opposed to τὰ πεζά and τὰ πλωτά, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 488a1, compare 542a23: hence πταναὶ θῆραι}} the pursuit **of winged game**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1146 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἡ [θήρα] τῶν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 823b}}: of young birds, **fledged**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 146 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **swift-footed**, παρδάλεις, κάμηλοι, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 219.5, 1402.3.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, πτηνοὶ μῦθοι, Homer's ἔπεα πτερόεντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1176: but κοῦφοι καὶ π. λόγοι}} **fleeting, idle** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 717d; π. ὄνειροι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 571}}; πτηνὰς διώκεις ἐλπίδας **fleeting** hopes, {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 271.}}
**II.2** πτανὰ ἰσχύς **soaring, aspiring** strength, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 107.3.}}

4422:
** πτοέω**
and **πτοιέω**, _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.214 (1st c.BC: Archias): _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ ἐπτοίησα, _Aeolic dialect_ ἐπτόαισα (see. below):—_passive_, _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ ἐπτοιήθην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 191:}}_perfect_ ἐπτόημαι, _Epic dialect_ ἐπτοίημαι (see. below):—**terrify, scare,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca}} [same place]:—_passive_, **to be scared, dismayed**, φρένες ἐπτοίηθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.298; ἐξ ὕπνου κέκραγεν ἐπτοημένη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 535}}; ἐπτοημένας δεινοῖς δράκουσιν by serpents, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1255 (assuming variant); ἔβαλλε χεῖρας ἐπτοημένας [prev. author] “Tr.” 559 (Lyric poetry); πτοηθεὶς ἐπὶ τοῖς ἠγγελμένοις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.11.4, cf. LXX.Exo.19.16, +others, NT.Luke.21.9, 24.37; περὶ ὃ ἂν τύχῃ Polystr. p.29}} W.; of animals, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 11.48, 13.457.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **flutter, excite** by any passion, τό μοι καρδίαν.. ἐπτόαισεν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.6, compare eand. “Supp.” 14.6; τὴν δὲ φρένας ἐπτοίησεν Κύπρις 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1232}}; Κύπρις ἐπ᾽ Αἰακίδῃ κούρῃ φρένας ἐπτοίησεν poetical cited in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 21.2}}:—_passive_, **to be passionately excited**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 5.2 (= 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1018); ἐπτοημένοι φρένας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 856; ὡς ἐπτόηται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 214, compare “IA” 1029; ἔρωτι ἐπτοάθης [prev. work] 586 (Lyric poetry)}}; πτοιηθεὶς ὑπ᾽ ἔρωτι Call.[same place]; τὸ περὶ τὰς ἐπιθυμίας μὴ ἐπτοῆσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 68c, compare “R.” 439d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 465; περὶ τὴν ὀχείαν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 614a26, compare 571b10; περὶ τὰ ὄψα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1128b; περὶ τὸ κέρδος 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 1.20; ἐς γυναῖκας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 5}}; ἐπὶ τὸ νέον{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 23; ἐπὶ γυναικί 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 4.2; πρὸς τὰς αἶγας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.989a; τῇ γνώμῃ πρὸς τὸν πόλεμον [prev. author] “Sull.” 7}}; **to be distraught**, μεθ᾽ ὁμήλικας ἐπτοίηται **he gapes like one distraught** after his fellows, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 447}}; τὸ πτοηθέν **distraction**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1268. (πτοι-}} only in dactylic verse, perhaps for the sake of meter; the -άω inflexion only in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus [same place], 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 586 (Lyric poetry)}}; Lesb. -αις (ε) may have -αι- for -η- as αἰμίονος, etc.)

4427:
** πτύσμα**, ατος, τό,
**sputum**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.11: more frequently in _plural_, [prev. author] “Acut.” 42, “Coac.” 401, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.12.5.}}
**II** serpent's **venom**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 3.9.}}

4428:
** πτύσσω**
(never πτύττω), (ἀνα-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 301:}}_future_ πτύξω (ἀνα-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1256:}}_aorist_ ἔπτυξα (see. below):—_middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.77}} (ποτι-), etc.: _future_ πτύξομαι (προσ-) {{Refs|3.22:}}_aorist_ ἐπτυξάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 267}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.134, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἐπτύχθην (ἀν-, δι-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.5, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 709:}}_aorist 2_ ἐπτύγην [ῠ], (ἀν-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 48}}: _perfect_ ἔπτυγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.72, etc., (ἀν-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 357; πέπτυκται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 536a11:}}_pluperfect_ ἔπτυκτο (προσ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 2.39}}:—**fold, double up**, χιτῶνα, εἵματα πτύξαι, **fold up** garments and put them by, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.439, 6.111, 252; σπλῆνα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 8}}; χεῖρας πτύξαι ἐπί τινι **fold** one's arms over or round another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1611}}; βιβλίον **fold, close** a book, {{NT|NT.Luke.4.20.}}
**II** _passive_, of the foetus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.69}}; of bandages, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).826; γραμματεῖα ἐπτυγμένα 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.17.1}}; πύργοι ἐπτ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus [same place]}}; ἔγχεα δὲ πτύσσοντο perhaps **were interlaced**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [prev. cited]}}
**II.2** **fold** or **cling round**, χιτὼν.. ἀμφὶ μηρὸν πτύσσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 872.3 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** _middle_, **fold round oneself, wrap round one**, τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 267.}}

4429:
** πτύω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.697, etc.:}}_future_ πτύσω [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.40}}; πτύσομαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Morb.” 1.28:}}_aorist_ ἔπτῠσα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 22, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 653, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἔπτῠκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.252, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.374, 11.281:}} —_passive_, _future_ πτυσθήσομαι (ἀνα-) {{NT|[prev. author] 9.686, 15.700, (ἐμ-) NT.Luke.18.32}}: _aorist_ ἐπτύσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 1.28}}: _aorist 2_ _participle_ πτυέντα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Epid.” 2.3.4}}. [ῡ in _present_ and _imperfect_; later _Epic dialect_ have ῠ in _imperfect_ before a short syllable, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 10.171}}, but mostly in compounds, (ἀνα-) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.570, (ἀπο-) 4.925, (ἐξ-) 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.19}}; ῠ always in _aorist_): —**spit out** or **up**, αἷμα Il.[same place], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 49}}: _absolutely_, **spit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.99, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.42: with _dative_, πτύσας προσώπῳ}} **spat** in his face, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1232.}}
**2** of the sea, **disgorge, cast out**, με.. τηλόσ᾽ ἀπὸ.. ἠϊόνος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.283 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus), compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 5.596: _metaphorically_, στοργὰν ἔπτυσας εἰς ἀνέμους “Anthologia Graeca” 7.468 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus): _absolutely_, ἐπ᾽ ἀϊόνι πτύοντα, of waves, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.133; ἱστὸς ὄλισθεν εἰς ἅλα πτύσας}}**with a splash,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.290 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**2.b** of fish, **spawn**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 6.8.}}
**2.c** proverbial, πρὶν πτύσαι 'before you can say Jack Robinson', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 202.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, πτύσας **with loathing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 653; ἰδεῖν ῥᾷόν ἐστι καὶ πτύσαι 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 3.20.}}
**4** εἰς κόλπον π., to avert a bad omen, disarm magic, etc., which was done three times, ὡς μὴ βασκανθῶ δὲ τρὶς εἰς ἐμὸν ἔπτυσα κόλπον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.39, compare 20.11; φρίξας εἰς κόλπον πτύσαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 16.15, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 15, etc.}}; ὑπὸ κόλπον π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.229 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** **promote the flow of spittle**, of certain wines, in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.52}}. (Cf. Gothic _speiwan_ 'spit', Latin _spuo_, etc.)

4430:
** πτῶμα**, ατος, τό,
(πίπτω) **fall**, πεσεῖν.. πτώματ᾽ οὐκ ἀνασχετά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 919; πίπτουσι.. πτώματ᾽ αἰσχρά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1046; π. θανάσιμον πεσῇ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 686}}; ἡ πόλις οὐκ ἂν ἔπεσε τοιοῦτον π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 181b.}}
**b** **fall** in wrestling, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.274, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.391 (3rd c.BC(?): Diotimus Epigrammaticus)}}: _metaphorically_, **booby-trap**, π. φιλοσόφων ἀπαλαιστρότατον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.8S.}}; **lapse, blunder**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.124.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **misfortune, calamity**, τά γ᾽ ἐκ θεῶν πτώματα **calamities** sent by the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1228.}}
**3** _plural_, **injuries due to falls, bruises**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.128,3.1,5.117.}}
**II** **fallen body, corpse, carcase**, frequently with _genitive_, πτῶμα Ἑλένης, Ἐτεοκλέους, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1196, “Ph.” 1697, cf. LXX.Judg.14.8, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.70, etc.; πτώματα νεκρῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1482 (_anapaest meter_)}}: without a _genitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 662 (assuming variant, Lyric poetry), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.14.2, “Sardis” 7 No. 165, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 33, etc.}}: collective in _singular_, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 700.17 (1st c.AD(?): Macedonius Epigrammaticus, 2nd c.BC), NT.Rev.11.8, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 6.18.1.}}
**II.2** of buildings, **ruin**, οἰκίας, κρηνῖδος, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11(2).161 A 120, 163 “Ba” 21 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἐπὶ τοῦ π. on the **ruins** (of the wall), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.31.8, compare 5.4.9, 5.100.6, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 25(43).27}}; **breach** in a city-wall, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.8, +others}}: _plural_, **ruins,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 11(2).199 A 103 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 100.45, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.28.2; π. οἴκων 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 351}}; π. ἐλαιῶν **fallen** olive-trees or fruit, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 203 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; **windfall fruit**, of the φοῖνιξ, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.109.}}
**III** **payment which falls due,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Elephantine Papyri 11.4 (3rd c.BC, _plural_), “PLond.” 1.3.37 (2nd c.BC).}}

4431:
** πτῶσις**, εως, ἡ,
(πίπτω, πέ-πτωκα) **falling, fall**, κύβων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604c, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.282 (_plural_); κεραυνῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 339a3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1005b (_plural_); Φαέθοντος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.16.13: _metaphorically_, π. τῆς ψυχῆς, opposed to ἔπαρσις, 4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.51 (_plural_)}}: _absolutely_, **calamity**, ἐν καιρῷ πτώσεως {{LXX+5th c.AD+|LXX.Sir.3.31, cf. LXX.Exo.30.12; πτώσεις ἀνθρώπων 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 251, +others}}; **death**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Ostentis” 12, 14, 20.}}
**II** Grammars, **mode** or **modification** of a word, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1457a18, +others}}; applied to cases, including _nominative_,{{Refs|[prev. work] 20, “Int.” 16b1: to genders, [prev. author] “SE” 173b27}}: to _superlative_ of _adjectives_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Top.” 136b30: to _adverbs_, [prev. work] 15, “Rh.” 1397a20: to _adjectives_ derived from nouns, e.g. χαλκοῦς, [prev. work] 1410a32: to tenses (except the _present_), [prev. author] “Int.” 16b17}}: so by the Stoics to variety of flexion, {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.263, but most frequently to cases of the Noun, περὶ τῶν πέντε π}}., title of work by Chrysipp.,{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|compare 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.16, etc.}}; κατὰ μίαν πτῶσιν **indeclinable**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 165.10.}}
**III** in the Logic of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}, **mood** of syllogisms, {{Refs|“APr.” 42b30.}}
**III.b** **arrangement of terms** in a syllogism, {{Refs|[prev. author] “APo.” 94a12.}}

4432:
** πτωχ-εία**,
_Ionic dialect_ **πτωχ-ηΐη**, ἡ, **beggary, mendicity**, ἐς πτωχηΐην ἀπῖκται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14; εἰς ἐσχάτην π. ἐλθεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936b; εἰς π. καταστάντες 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.10: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 618a: proverbial, πτωχείας πενία ἀδελφή 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 549.}}
**II** **poor relief,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.3.41.23. -εῖον}}, τό, **poorhouse**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|[prev. work]1.2.15.1 (_plural_), 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Aedificiis” 5.9, “Etymologicum Magnum” 187.22}}; written **πτωχῖον**, {{Refs|“MAMA” 3.783}}; Latin _ptochium,_ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 1.3.48.1.}}

4433:
** πτωχ-εύω**,
_future_ **-σω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.309}}: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ πτωχεύεσκον {{Refs|18.2}}:—**to be a beggar, go begging**, [prev. cited]; ἀνὰ δῆμον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|19.73, compare 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 10.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 921 (_anapaest meter_), etc.; ἐπὶ ξενίας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.9.}}
**2** **to be as poor as a beggar**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 322, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Eryxias” 394b.}}
**3** _metaphorically_ with _genitive_, **to be badly off for**, πραγμάτων, of historians, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.7.6.}}
**II** _transitive_, **beg** (**for**), δαῖτα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.11, 19.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _person_, **ask an alms of**, φίλους {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 922.}}

4434:
** πτωχός**, ή, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1274, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 444, 751}}:— **beggar**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.400, 18.1, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.14, etc.; πρὸς γὰρ Διός εἰσιν ἅπαντες ξεῖνοί τε πτωχοί τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.208, compare 17.475; πτωχὸς πτωχῷ φθονέει 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 26; π. ἀνὴρ ἀλαλήμενος ἐλθών 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.327; π. καὶ ἀλήμονες ἄνδρες 19.74; πτωχοὺς ἀλᾶσθαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 515; πτωχοῦ βίος ζῆν ἐστιν μηδὲν ἔχοντα, τοῦ δὲ πένητος ζῆν φειδόμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 552: proverbial, πτωχοῦ πήρη οὐ πίμπλαται 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 360: πτωχή}} **beggar-woman**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 10.453a (so πτωχός (_feminine_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 444); χήρα πτωχή NT.Mark.12.42.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, οἱ π. τῷ πνεύματι {{NT|NT.Matt.5.3, cf. NT.Luke.6.20.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **beggarly**, πτωχῷ διαίτῃ {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 751; π. στοιχεῖα NT.Gal.4.9}}: with _genitive_, **beggared of, poor in**, [πηγὴ] π. νυμφῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.258 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.2** _comparative_ πτωχότερος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 6.10}}; proverbial, π. κίγκλου 'as poor as a church mouse', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 221; irregular πτωχίστερος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 425}}: _superlative_ πτωχότατος {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 10.50 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}
**II.3** _adverb_ -χῶς **poorly, scantily**, ἠροτρία π. μέν, ἀλλ᾽ ἀναγκαίως {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 55.2.}}

4435:
** πυγμ-ή**, ἡ,
(πύξ) **fist**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 71, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1368; τῇ π. θενών 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1384; πυγμῇ πατάξαι LXX.Exo.21.18, cf. LXX.Isa.58.4.}}
**2** **boxing**, as an athletic contest, πυγμῇ νικήσαντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.669; πυγμὴν νικᾶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1031; ἄνδρας πυγμὰν ἐνίκα Ὀλύμπια “Anthologia Graeca” 6.256 (Antip.); πυγμᾶς ἄποινα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.16, compare 10(11).67}}; πυγμὴν or τὴν π. ἀσκεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 795b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 61.24}}; frequently in Inscrr., e.g. πυγμὴν Ζωΐλος (i.e. ἐνίκησε) {{Refs|“IG” 7.1765 (from Thespiae), etc.}}
**2.b** generally, **fight**, π. μονομάχων καὶ θηρίων Edict.Caes. cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 14.10.6, compare 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 5.58}}; εἰς π. καθίστασθαι, τρέπεσθαι, of partridges, {{Refs|“Geoponica” 14.20.1,2.}}
**3** in {{NT|NT.Mark.7.3, πυγμῇ νίψασθαι}} is interpretation **diligently** (variant{πυκνά}, **often**).
**II** a measure of length, **the distance from the elbow to the knuckles**,= {{Refs 4th c.BC+|18 δάκτυλοι, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.11.5, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.147, 158.}}

4436:
** Πύθων** [ῡ], ωνος, ὁ,
(compare Πυθώ) the serpent “Python”, slain by Apollo, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus 31 (b) 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 1.4.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.293c.}}
**II** παιδίσκη ἔχουσα πνεῦμα Πύθωνα a spirit **of divination,** {{NT|NT.Acts.16.16.}}
**II.2** _plural_ Πύθωνες, **ventriloquists**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.414e, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

4437:
** πυκνός**, ή, όν,
_poetry_ also **πῠκῐνός**, ή, όν, both forms in _Epic dialect_ (see. below) and Lyric poetry, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.52 (_superlative_), 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 1}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **πύκνος** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.11, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9 (πύκινος}} is uncertain reading {{Refs|[prev. author] 82)}}; Trag. **πυκνός**, except {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus in Lyric poetry, “Aj.” 1208, “Ph.” 854}}; πυκινός once in Comedy texts, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 38 (assuming variant): _Laconian dialect_ _superlative_ πουκότατος is corrupt in 3rd c.BC: Simmias 26.17}}:— **close, compact.**
**I** of a thing with reference to the close union of its parts, **close, firm, solid**, πυκινὸς θώρηξ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.529; χλαῖναν πυκνὴν καὶ μεγάλην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.521; πυκινὸν νέφος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.751}}; πυκινὸν λέχος **well-stuffed, firm** bed,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|9.621, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.340; πυκνὸν καὶ μαλακόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.349; Ἁρμονίης πυκινῷ κρυφῷ 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 27.3; σπάρτα πυκνὰ ἐστραμμένα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.15; π. δέμας 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.59}}; of a sponge, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑλκῶν” 2; π. ὀστοῦν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 75b, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 22}}; [σάρκες] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 74e}}; χρυσοῦ πυκνότερον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 59b; ἔβενος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.5.5; πλεύμων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.698b; χωρία [prev. work]650d; πυκινὴν νάπαις Ἄζιλιν 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Apollinem” 89}}; [ὁ ἐλαιὼν] πυκνός ἐστι τοῖς φυτοῖς **overgrown** with plants, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PFay.” 113.8 (1st-2nd c.AD)}}; ξοῒς χαρακτὴ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 7.3073.104 (Lebad., 2nd c.BC)}}; of a woman, **thick-set, stocky**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.34.}}
**I.2** **narrow, constricted**, οὐ διέρχεται.. ἀρκέουσα ἰκμάς.., πυκνῆς τῆς ὁδοῦ ἐούσης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.73; πυκνοὺς ἔχουσι τοὺς πόρους τοῦ σώματος 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 1.6.}}
**II** of the parts of a thing, **close-packed, crowded**, πυκιναὶ κίνυντο φάλαγγες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.281; τῶν δὲ στίχες ἥατο πυκναί 7.61, etc.; πυκινὸν λόχον εἷσαν 4.392, etc.(see. below 111.1); πυκνὰ καρήατα λαῶν 11.309; πυκνοὶ ἐφέστασαν ἀλλήλοισιν 13.133, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.480; σταυροῖσιν πυκινοῖσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.453; σταυροὺς.. πυκνοὺς καὶ θαμέας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.12}}; of **thick** plumage, πυκινὰ πτερά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.53; πτερὰ πυκνά 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.454, 23.879}}; but πύκνα πτέρα **fast-beating** wings, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 1.11 (and so perhaps 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus [same places])}}; frequently of thick foliage, ὕλη, λόχμη, θάμνοι, ὄζοι, ῥωπήϊα, δρυμά, πέταλα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.320, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.439, 5.471, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.245, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.473, 10.150, 19.520; π. νέφεα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 553}}; πυκινοῖσι λίθοισι with **close-laid** stones, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.212}}; πυκινοῖσι.. βελέεσσι with **a thick shower of** darts,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.576; πυκνῆσιν λιθάδεσσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.36; τοξεύματα πολλὰ καὶ π. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.218}}; πυκνοῖς ὄσσοις δεδορκώς, of Argus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 678}}; πεπλεκτανημέναι π. δράκουσιν, of the Furies, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 1050}}; of **thick-falling** rain, snow, etc., πυκνῆς ἀκοῦσαι ψακάδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 636; πυκιναῖς δρόσοις [prev. author] “Aj.” 1208 (Lyric poetry); πυκνῇ νιφάδι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1129}}; π. ῥόος a **dense** current, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 100.14; π. θρίξ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.6; π. τρίχες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321a}}; [δένδρεα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.22, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.2; τὰ μὲν π... τὰ δὲ μανὰ κατὰ τὴν φυτείαν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.8.2.}}
**II.b** in Tactics, **in close order**, opposed to ἀραιός, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 4.1 (_superlative_), 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 11.1 (_comparative_).}}
**II.2** of a repeated action, **frequent, numerous**, πυκνοὺς θεοπρόπους ἴαλλε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 658; τῶν π. φιλημάτων [prev. author] “fragment” 135; ὀδύναι πυκνόταται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 22; πυκινῶν κρεγμῶν ἀκροαζομένα 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 109 (_anapaest meter_); π. ὁδοὺς ἐλθόντα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 235}}; π. βαίνων ἤλυσιν, of a blind man, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 844}}; ἐν πυκνῷ θεοῦ τροχῷ κυκλεῖται on the **oft-revolving** wheel, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 871.1; μεταβολαὶ πυκνόταται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 13; πνεῦμα πυκνότερον}} **quicker** breathing, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Acut.” 16; π. σφυγμὸς ἢ μανός 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.136f}}; **continuous, constant**, φῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Corpus Hermeticum” 16.10; ἐρωτήμασι πυκνοῖς χρώμενοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44; ἡ.. εἰωθυῖά μοι μαντικὴ.. πάνυ πυκνὴ ἦν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 40a; ἐπιθυμίαι π. τε καὶ σφοδραί [prev. author] “R.” 573e; τὰς ἐντεύξεις π. ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.20: with}}_infinitive_, πυκνοτέραν εἰσαφικνεῖσθαι πᾶσιν ἀνθρώποις ποιεῖν τὴν πόλιν **more frequently** visited by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 5.1 codices}}
**III** of artificial union, **well put together, compact, strong**, πυκινὸς δόμος, χηλός, θύραι, θάλαμος, κευθμῶνες (see. below {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 111.1), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.267, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.68, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.167, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.229, 10.283; ἀσπὶς ῥινοῖσιν πυκινή 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.804; π. δῶμα 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 17}}: hence, **close, concealed**, πυκινὸς δόλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.187; and so perhaps π. λόχος, see above 11.1.}}
**III.2** in Music, πυκνόν, τό, part of the tetrachord in which the intervals are small, defined as τὸ ἐκ δύο διαστημάτων συνεστηκὸς ἃ συντεθέντα ἔλαττον διάστημα περιέξει τοῦ λειπομένου διαστήματος ἐν τῷ διὰ τεσσάρων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoxenus Musicus “Harmonica” p.24M., compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1135b, etc.}}
**IV** generally, **strong** of its kind, **sore, excessive**, ἄτη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.480; μελεδῶναι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.516; ἄχος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.599.}}
**V** _metaphorically_ of the mind, **shrewd, wise**, πυκιναὶ φρένες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|14.294, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus [same place]; νόος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.461; μήδεα 3.208; βουλή 2.55; ἐφετμή 18.216; μῦθοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.23; ἔπος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.788}}; θυμός, βουλαί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.73, “I.” 7(6).8; φρήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 67}}; μήτιδι πυκνῇ Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.141, compare “IG” 3.1320: in Prose, πυκνὴ διάνοια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 568a}}; τὸ π. **terseness** of expression, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Thucydide” 24.}}
**V.2** of persons, **sagacious, shrewd, crafty, cunning**, Σίσυφος πυκνότατος παλάμαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.52; κύων πυκινώτατον ἑρπετόν [prev. author] “fragment” 106}}; πυκινοί **the wise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 854 (Lyric poetry); πυκνότατον κίναδος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 430 (Lyric poetry); πυκνός τις καὶ σοφὸς γνώμην ἀνήρ 5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 25.12.}}
**B** _adverb_ πυκινῶς, and after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus πυκνῶς, θύραι or σανίδες πυκινῶς ἀραρυῖαι}} **close** or **fast shut**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.475, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.344, etc.}}
**B.2** **sorely** (see. above {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus IV), πυκινῶς ἀκαχήμενος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.312, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.95, +others}}; **constantly**, ὅταν π. διᾴττωσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.22.}}
**B.3** **sagaciously, shrewdly**, π. ὑποθήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.279, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.293; πυκνῶς ἀνευρεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 438 (Lyric poetry, assuming variant).}}
**B.II** _neuter_ _singular_ and _plural_, πυκνόν, πυκνά, πυκινόν, πυκινά as _adverb_, especially in the sense **much, often**, πήρην πυκνὰ ῥωγαλέην a **much** torn wallet, a wallet full of holes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.438, 17.198; πυκινόν περ ἀχεύων 11.88; τέττιξ.. καταχεύετ᾽ ἀοιδὴν πυκνόν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 584: in Prose, πυκνὰ ἐκπίπτει ὦμος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 2; πυκνὰ ἀποβλέπειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 501b; πυκνὰ στρέφεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.8; πυκνὸν ἀναπνεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1357b19}}; πυκνότερον ἰέναι, παρέρχεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 328d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.24; πυκνότερα ἐπάγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 420d}}. _adverb_ **-οτέρως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lesbonax Grammaticus 23, “PLond.” 5.1929 (4th c.AD)}}: _superlative_ πυκνότατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.11.}}
**B.II.2** πυκινὰ φρονεῖν (see. supr. A.V) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.445.}}
**B.III** _poetry_ _adverb_ **πύκα** [??], **thickly, solidly**, θαλάμου πύκα ποιητοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.436; π. π. δόμοιο 22.455; σάκεος π. π. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18. 608; Λυκίων π. θωρηκτάων 12.317, compare 15.689, 739}}; πύλαι π. στιβαρῶς ἀραρυῖαι {{Refs|12.454.}}
**B.III.2** θάλαμος πύκ᾽ ἐβάλλετο with **thick-falling** darts, {{Refs|9.588.}}
**B.III.3** **wisely**, π. φρονεῖν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. work] 554, 14.217, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.449, +others}}; τρέφειν rear **carefully**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.70.}}

4439:
** πύλη** [ῠ], ἡ,
properly **one wing of a pair of double gates**, ὀλίγον τι παρακλίναντες τὴν ἑτέρην π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.156}}: mostly in _plural_, **gates of a town** (whereas θύρα = house-door), Σκαιαὶ π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.145, etc.; π. εὖ ἀραρυίας 7.339; πύκα στιβαρῶς ἀραρυίας 12.454; πεπταμένας ἐν χερσὶ π. ἔχετε 21.531}}; ἄνεσάν τε π. καὶ ἀπῶσαν ὀχῆας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 537; π. ἀνοῖξαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 604; π. κλῇσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 560c (the _Article_ is frequently omitted even in Prose)}}: _plural_ of several **gates**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 125}}; ἐν πύλαις in or at **the gates**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 160, 213 (both Lyric poetry), +others; πρὸς πύλαις [prev. work] 377, 457}}; ἐπὶ ταῖς πύλαισιν, οὗ τὸ τάριχος ὤνιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1247.}}
**2** in Trag. sometimes of the **house-door**, δωμάτων πύλαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 732, compare 561}}; γυναικείους π. **gate** or **door** leading to the women's apartments,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 878; πύλαις διπλαῖς ἐνήλατο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1261; ἐκτὸς αὐλείων πυλῶν [prev. author] “Ant.” 18}}: also in _singular_,{{Refs|[prev. work] 1186, “El.” 818}}; of the **door** of a tent, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 11; πύλης ἄναξ θυρωρέ [prev. author] “fragment” 775.}}
**3** πύλαι Ἀΐδαο the **gates of the nether world**, _periphrastic_for **hell**, {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.646, 9.312, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.156; Ἅιδου πύλαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1291, cf. NT.Matt.16.18, etc.; also σκότου πύλαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1; νερτέρων π. [prev. author] “Hipp.” 1447.}}
**4** **custom-house,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 5.34 (2nd c.BC)}}; τετελώνηται διὰ πύλης has paid the **customs**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“BGU” 1592 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; τὸ σύμβολον τῆς ἱερᾶς Συηνιτικῆς π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PStrassb.” 79.10 (1st c.BC)}}; μισθωταὶ ἱερᾶς π. Σοήνης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Ostr.” 106 (2nd c.AD), +others,compare “Ostr.Bodl.” see C 1 (2nd c.AD)}}
**II** generally, **entrance, orifice**, ἀμφὶ πύλας ἰσθμοῖο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 100.19; ἀναπεπταμένας ἔχω τῶν ὤτων τὰς π. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.169a; πύλας τοῖς ὠσὶν ἐπίθεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218b}}; ἡλίου πύλαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus name for the eyes, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.29}}; **portal fissure** of the liver, π. καὶ δοχαὶ χολῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 828, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 2.4.1, “Anatomy texts” 1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 71c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 496b32, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.145}}; **portal vein** of the liver, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 179, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.785,5.542.}}
**II.b** _plural_, of the **carceres** in the circus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.124J.}}
**II.c** _metaphorically_, πύλας ὕμνων ἀναπιτνάμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.27; ἐπέων π. 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus “fragment” 4; ἐν πύλαις γήρως 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 57.24, compare 76.7.}}
**II.2** **entrance into a country through mountains, pass**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.52: hence, Πύλαι, αἱ, the common name for Θερμοπύλαι}}, **the Gates** of Greece, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.201, etc.}}: of other **passes**, π. τῆς Κιλικίας καὶ τῆς Συρίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.4; αἱ Σύριαι π. [prev. work] 5}}; αἱ Κάσπιαι π. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 11.12.1; π. Λύδιαι [prev. author] 13.1.65}}; Ἀμανίδες π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 14.5.18, 16.2.8 (αἱ Ἀμανικαὶ καλούμεναι 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.7.1)}}: these **passes** were sometimes really barred by **gates**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.176, compare 3.117, 5.52, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus [same place]: the Isthmus is called πόντοιο πύλαι, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.27; Κορίνθου π. [prev. author] “O.” 9.86; αἱ π. τῆς Πελοποννήσου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 2.17}}; Πέλοπος νάσου θεόδματοι π{{Refs 1st c.AD+|. B. p.437 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**II.3** of **narrow straits**, by which one enters a broad sea, Πύλαι Γαδειρίδες the {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Straits” of Gibraltar, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 256}}; ἐπ᾽ αὐταῖς στενοπόροις λίμνης π., of the Thracian Bosporus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 729}}; ἐν πύλαις, of the Euripus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 803.}}

4441:
** πυνθάνομαι**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.315, etc.}}; _poetry_ also **πεύθομαι** (which see): _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ πυνθανόμην {{Refs|13.256}}: _future_ πεύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.19, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ πευσοῦμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.51}} (so codex _middle_ in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 988)}}: _aorist_ ἐπῠθόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5. 702}}, etc., _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry πυθόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.732, 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 15.26}}; _imperative_ πυθοῦ, _Ionic dialect_ πυθεῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.68}}; _Epic dialect_ _optative_ πεπύθοιτο [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.50, +others (_subjunctive_ πεπύθωνται is _falsa lectio_ for{γε πύθωνται}, 7.195)}}]; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πυθοίατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 921}}: _perfect_ πέπυσμαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.505, etc.}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ πέπῠσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310b}}, _Epic dialect_ πέπυσσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.494}}; _infinitive_ πεπύσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67, etc.}}; _participle_ πεπυσμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 179e}}: _pluperfect_ ἐπεπύσμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 615, “Av.” 470}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐπέπυστο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.674, _Epic dialect_ πέπυστο [prev. work] 521}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ πεπύσθην {{Refs|17.377}}:—**learn**, whether **by hearsay** or **by inquiry** (ἐξιστορήσαντες τὰ ἐβούλοντο πυθέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.195): _construction_}}
**1** π. τί τινος **learn** something **from** a person, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.408, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.537, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 599, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1417, etc.; also π. τι ἀπό τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 737; ἐξ ἄλλων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1266; ἐκ τοῦ παρατυχόντος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22}}; frequently παρά τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.91, etc.}}; παρ᾽ ἄλλων (variant{ἄλλων}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.3.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_ only, **hear** or **learn** a thing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.411, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 765, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.25, etc.: _absolutely_, αἰσχρὸν τόδε γ᾽ ἐστὶ καὶ ἐσσομένοισι πυθέσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.119, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 7.7, etc.; ὡς ἐγὼ πυνθάνομαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22, etc.}}
**3** with _genitive_ objecti, **hear** or **inquire concerning**, πυθέσθαι πατρός, ἀγγελιάων, μάχης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.281, 2.256, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.224, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 35, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 635b.}}
**4** π. τινά τινος **inquire about** one person **of** or **from** another, τὸν ἄνδρα τῶν ὁδοιπόρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 204; so π. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.75; πᾶσαν πυθέσθαι τῶνδ᾽ ἀλήθειαν πέρι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 91}}: with _accusative_ _person_ only, **inquire about** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 619.}}
**5** with _participle_, πυθόμην ὁδὸν ὁρμαίνοντα that he was starting, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.732, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.58, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 692; π. τὸ Πλημμύριον ἑαλωκός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.16, etc.}}; οὔ πω.. πεπύσθην Πατρόκλοιο θανόντος they **had** not yet **heard of** his being dead, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.377, compare 427, 19.322, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 763; ὡς ἐπύθοντο τῆς Πύλου κατειλημμένης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.6: with _accusative_ _of things_ added, εἰ σφῶϊν τάδε πάντα πυθοίατο μαρναμένοιιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.257.}}
**6** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **hear** or **learn that.. **, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62, 5.15, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 103 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25, etc.}}
**7** followed by an _interrogative_ clause, ὡς πυθώμεθα ὅπου ποτ᾽ ἐσμέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 11}}; αὐτοῦ π. τί ποτε νοεῖ **inquire** or **learn** from him what.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.25, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.107.6; π., ὅτεῳ.. συνοικέει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.68}}; π. εἰ.. **inquire** whether.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 993, “IG” 42(1).121.18 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; τοῦ ξένου ἡδέως ἂν π., τί ταῦθ᾽ ἡγοῦντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 216d}}; π. τινῶν, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.17}}; π., ὅπως ἂν κάλλιστα πορευθείη{{Refs|[prev. work] 3.1.7, compare “Cyr.” 1.4.7.}}
**II** _active_, _aorist 1_ _participle_ _feminine_ πεύσασα **having learned,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PMasp. 5.7 (6th c.AD).}}
**II.2** see at {πεύθω}.

4442:
** πῦρ**
(once **πύυρ** [?~X ¯ ?~X] by 'distraction', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 59 codices Hdn.Gr. (“Rh.Mus.” 35.101, 38.378)), τό, _genitive_ πῠρός}}; not used in _plural_, see at {πυρά},{τά}:—**fire**, π. καίειν or δαίειν to kindle **fire**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.521, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.7, etc.}}; π. ἀνακαίειν, ἅπτειν, ἐξάπτειν, αἴθειν, ἐναύειν, (see entry).; π. ποιεῖν, ποιήσασθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.27; οἴσετε π. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.718; π. προσέφερον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; π. ἐμβαλεῖν νηυσί, κλισίῃσι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.597, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.501 (tmesis)}}; π. φυσητέον, ῥιπίζειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 293, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 21; as exclamation, πῦρ βοᾷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 208.}}
**b** π. τεχνικόν, see at {τεχνικός}.
**2** **funeral-fire** (compare πυρά), ὄφρα πυρός με.. λελάχωσι θανόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.79, 22.342, compare 15.350, 23.76}}; ζῶντα διδόναι τινὰ πυρί **burn** one alive, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86.}}
**3** **sacrificial fire**, ἐν πυρὶ βάλλε θυηλάς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.220, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.341, 446; κατὰ τοῦ π. σπένδειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 120a; διὰ τοῦ π. ὀμνύναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 54.40.}}
**4** **hearthfire**, πυρὸς ἐσχάραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.418, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.59; ἕως ἂν αἴθῃ πῦρ ἐφ᾽ ἑστίας ἐμῆς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1435; πυρὶ δέχεσθαί τινα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 47}}; τὸ π. τὸ ἀθάνατον **the fire** of Vesta, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 9, etc.; deified, Πῦρ ἀθάνατον “SIG” 826 ii 14 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}
**5** **lightning**, κεραυνὸς ἀενάου πυρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.6; πῦρ πνέοντος κεραυνοῦ [prev. author] “fragment” 146; πυρὸς ἀμφήκης βόστρυχος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1044 (_anapaest meter_); πυρὶ καὶ στεροπαῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 470 (_anapaest meter_); παλτῷ ῥιπτεῖ πυρί [prev. author] “Ant.” 131 (_anapaest meter_); θείῳ πυρὶ παμφαής [prev. author] “Ph.” 728 (Lyric poetry).}}
**5.b** **fire, light**, or **heat** of the sun, θερινὸν π., opposed to χειμών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.50, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 865b}}; of the stars, π. πνείοντα ἄστρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1146 (Lyric poetry)}}; **summer solstice**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 79, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “A.” 3.}}
**6** **flame** of torches, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 964 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}; π. εὐάγγελον, ἄγγαρον, πομπόν, of the beacon fire, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 21, 282, 299.}}
**7** **fever heat, violent fever**, πῦρ ἔλαβέν [τινα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.ή, +others}}; ὁ δ᾽ ἔχων θέρμαν καὶ π. ἧκεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 690; τεταρταίῳ πυρί 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.17; π. ἄγριον 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.20 (of erysipelas _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.134).}}
**II** phrases, ἐν πυρὶ γενέσθαι to be consumed, come to nothing, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.340}}; φεύγων καπνὸν εἰς πῦρ δεσποτείας ἐμπεπτωκώς 'out of the frying pan into the **fire**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 569b, cf. Prov. cited in 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Epictetum commentaria” p.72 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; ἦν ἄρα πυρός γ᾽ ἕτερα θερμότερα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 382}}; πῦρ ἐπὶ πῦρ ἐγχεῖν, ἄγειν, φέρειν, ἐπεισφέρειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 18, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 453, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 880a21, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.61a}}; εἰς π. ξαίνειν 'plough the sands', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 780c; βασανίζειν χρυσὸν ἐν πυρί [prev. author] “R.” 413e, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 21.20.7}}: as a type of things **irresistible** or **terrible**, ἀντίος εἶμι, καὶ εἰ πυρὶ χεῖρας ἔοικε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.371; μάρναντο δέμας πυρὸς αἰθομένοιο 11.596, +others; Ἕκτωρ πυρὸς αἰνὸν ἔχει μένος 17.565, compare 6.182; so τὸ πεπρωμένον οὐ π. σχήσει 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 232; κρεῖσσον ἀμαιμακέτου πυρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 177; οὐδὲν θηρίον γυναικὸς ἀμαχώτερον, οὐδὲ π. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1015; ἀναρχία κρείσσων πυρός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 608; ἐχίδνης καὶ πυρὸς περαιτέρω [prev. author] “Andr.” 271}}; so διὰ πυρὸς ἰέναι (as we say) to go through **fire and water**, dash through any danger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.16, compare “Oec.” 21.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 133}}; but διὰ πυρὸς ἦλθε ἑτέρῳ λέχεϊ she **raged furiously** against the other partner of the bed, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 487 (Lyric poetry); διὰ πυρὸς ἔμολον ματρί [prev. author] “El.” 1183 (Lyric poetry); σωθήσεται οὕτω δὲ ὡς διὰ πυρός NT.1Cor.3.15; εἰς π. ἅλλεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.9; κἂν εἰς π. ἐμβαῖεν 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 314.3; π. διέρπειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 265}}; of persons, ὦ π. σύ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 927}}; of Hannibal, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 21}}: _metaphorically_ of anxious hope, θάλπει τῷδ᾽ ἀνηκέστῳ πυρί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 888}}; of love, ἀρσενικῷ θέρεται π. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 27.5, compare 45.2}}. (Cf. Armenian _hur_, O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **fyr** 'fire', etc.)

4443:
** πῠρά**, ᾶς,
_Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ **πῠρή**, ῆς, ἡ, (πῦρ) **funeral-pyre**, πυραὶ νεκύων καίοντο θαμειαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.52, etc.; πυρῆς ἐπιβάντ᾽ ἀλεγεινῆς 4.99, etc.; ἐν δὲ πυρῇ ὑπάτῃ νεκρὸν θέσαν 23.165, 24.787, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.24, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1254, etc.; ποίησαν δὲ πυρὴν ἑκατόμπεδον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.164}}; πυρὴν νῆσαι, συννῆσαι, to raise **one**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50, 86; πυρῆς ἁμμένης [prev. passage]; τινὰ πυρᾷ κέαντες 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 757; σκῦλα πρὸς πυρὰν ἐμὴν κόμιζε [prev. author] “Ph.” 1432; ἐπὶ τῇ π. κείμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614b; αἱ τῶν ζωγρηθέντων σφαγαὶ εἰς τὴν π. [prev. work] 391b.}}
**b** **mound raised on the place of the pyre, tumulus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).63, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 901, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 386, “IT” 26.}}
**2** **altar for burnt sacrifice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.167, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1258 (troch.); ἑρκεῖος π. [prev. author] “Tr.” 483}}; **fire burning thereon**, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.39.}}
**3** **burning mass**,{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 107, 1st c.BC: Zosimus Epigrammaticus 2.13}}; λαμπάδων π. **a mass of burning** torches, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.36.}}

4444:
** πύργος**, ὁ,
**tower**, especially such as were attached to the walls of a city, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.219, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 242, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.74, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.17, +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.99.9, etc.}}: in _plural_, **city walls** or **ramparts with their towers**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.338, 437; in _sing._, ἧντ᾽ ἐπὶ πύργῳ 3.153, compare 22.447; πόλιος ἣν πέρι πύργος ὑψηλός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.262; πέριξ δὲ πύργος εἶχ᾽ ἔτι πτόλιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1209; πύργους ἐπὶ τῶν γεφυρῶν ἐπιστῆσαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 116a.}}
**b** **movable tower** for storming towns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.53, 6.2.18; π. ὑπότροχοι 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 42.3.}}
**c** **tower** on the back of war-elephants, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 2.4.}}
**d** Ζανὸς π., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for the central fire of the universe, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 204.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **tower of defence**, τοῖος.. σφιν π. ἀπώλεο, of Ajax, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.556; ἄνδρες πόλιος π. ἀρεύϊος 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 1a.10; παῖς ἄρσην πατέρ᾽ ἔχει π. μέγαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 311, compare “Med.” 390; ἅπας μοι π. Ἑλλήνων πατρίς “Tragica Adespota” 392}}; θανάτων δ᾽ ἐμᾷ χώρᾳ π. ἀνέστα **a tower of defence from** deaths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1201 (Lyric poetry).}}
**3** **the part of a house** (probably a separate building) **in which the women lived and worked**, αἱ ἄλλαι θεράπαιναι ἐν τῷ π. ἦσαν, οὗπερ διαιτῶνται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.56; especially if unmarried, as 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} in her **tower**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Musaeus Epicus 32, 187, compare 3rd c.AD: Philostratus Junior Sophista “Imagines” 1}}; of the **workman's hut** of Timon the misanthrope (which also became his tomb,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 42), 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.30.4, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 7.402 (Antip.)}}; **outbuildings**, especially if used in industry, {{LXX+3rd c.BC+|LXX.Isa.5.2, LXX.Mic.4.8, “PStrassb.” 110.6 (3rd c.BC), “BGU” 1194.9 (2nd c.BC), 650.8 (1st c.AD), “POxy.” 243.15 (1st c.AD), NT.Mark.12.1, NT.Luke.14.28, “PGiss.” 67.16 (2nd c.AD), “IG” 22.2776.65 (2nd c.AD)}}; π. ἐν ᾧ βαφεῖον καὶ ἕτερα χρηστήρια {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.371.3 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** **part of an army drawn up in close order, column**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.334, 347.}}
**II.2** at Teos, a division of the people, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3064,3081, +others}}
**III** **dice-box,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.482.24 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; cf. Latin _pyrgus._

4446:
** πῠρετ-ός**, ὁ,
(πῦρ) **burning heat, fiery heat**, φέρει πυρετὸν δειλοῖσι βροτοῖσιν (i.e. Sirius) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.31.}}
**II** **fever**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.26, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1038 (_plural_), etc.}}; θνήσκειν ἐκ π. {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 247 (from Mysia)}}; π. ἀμφημερινοί, τριταῖοι, τεταρταῖοι, quotidian, tertian, quartan **fevers**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 86a, etc. (see. _mentally supplied_ voccative); διαλείποντες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 866a23.}}

4447:
** πύρῐνος** [ῠ], η, ον,
(πῦρ) **of fire, fiery**, σῶμα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 435a12, compare “GC” 326a31}}; εἰ.. ὁ ἀὴρ μὴ πῦρ, ἀλλὰ π. {{NT+1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1049a26; ἄστρα [prev. author] “Cael.” 289a16; δοκίς 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.50; θώρακες NT.Rev.9.17; π. κλῇθρα “PMag.Par.” 1.589}}; π. νύμφαι **hot** springs, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 14.52}}; π. φάρμακον **fiery** drug, probably arsenic, Maria cited in Zos.Alch.p.201 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**II** _metaphorically_, π. πόλεμος **bitter, obstinate** war, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 35.1.6, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 31.40.}}
**II.2** π. ἀσπαστικόν **fiery** greeting, {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.638.}}

4448:
** πῠρόω**,
(πῦρ) **burn with fire, burn up**, τὰς Ἀθήνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.8.β, 8.102; στέγην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 281; ναούς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 286}}; **burn as a burnt sacrifice**, ὀσφῦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 497; πυροῦτε σώματα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 244}}; **burn on a pyre**, ἣν πεπύρωκαν (sic) ἐγώ {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.569 (from Egypt)}}; π. Κύκλωπος ὄψιν **burn out** his eye, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 594, compare 600:}} —_middle_, παῖδα πυρωσαμένη **having placed my** son **on the pyre,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 7.466 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus)}}:—_passive_, **to be set on fire, to be burnt**, πυρωθέντων Τρώων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.33; Ἴλιον πυρούμενον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 400, compare “Tr.” 1283; πυρωθῆναι δέμας [prev. author] “IT” 685, compare “Med.” 1190, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus in “PLit.Lond.” 64.6, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.256.}}
**b** π. τὴν γεῦσιν, τὴν γλῶσσαν, **seem hot to** the taste or tongue, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.16, 4.170.}}
**c** ἡ ζεστολουσία.. πυροῦσα τὴν ἐπιφάνειαν **reddening** or **warming up** the surface, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD(?): Theon Rhetor cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.208.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **set on fire, inflame**, Ἔρως σὺ δ᾽ εὐθέως με πύρωσον {{Refs|“Anacreontea” 10.15}}:—_passive_, **to be inflamed** or **excited**, παραγγέλμασιν.. πυρωθεὶς καρδίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 481 (Lyric poetry)}}; τινι by a person (with love), {{NT+1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.87; εὐχαριστίᾳ 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.60, cf. NT.2Cor.11.29.}}
**II** _absolutely_, **produce fire**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 649b5}}:—_passive_, **to become fire, to be ignited**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 51b, 52d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 307a24, +others}}
**III** **treat with fire: roast, grill**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.56}}; **bake**, πλίνθους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.420; τὸ σταῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 927b39, compare 929b12 (_passive_)}}; **warm on the fire**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Agatharchides Geographus 61 (_passive_)}}; **melt**, [ἀργυρώματα] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 7.303.15 (Oropus, 3rd c.BC)}}; **make red hot**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.625 (_passive_)}}; **fumigate**, δῶμα θεείῳ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.96}}; **cauterize**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 515b18 (_passive_).}}
**III.2** _passive_, **to be affected by fire**, ὁ χρυσὸς οὐ πυροῦται {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 378b4.}}
**III.3** of gold, **to be proved** or **tested by fire**, χρυσίον πεπυρωμένον ἐκ πυρός {{NT|NT.Rev.3.18}}: _metaphorically_, **proved by fire, approved**, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Psa.17(18).31, 118, 119.140, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.57.}}
**III.4** **to be affected by heart-burn**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 9.2.}}

4450:
** πυρρός**, ά
(_Ionic dialect_ ή), όν, but Trag. and _Doric dialect_ **πυρσός**, ή (_Doric dialect_ ά), όν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 32, “HF” 361 (Lyric poetry), 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.70, conjecture in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 316}}; rare in Prose, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 22}}: (πῦρ):— **flame-coloured, yellowish-red** (πυρρὸν ξανθοῦ τε καὶ φαιοῦ κράσει γίγνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 68c, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1054b13, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.600), ᾠοῦ τὸ π}}. the **yolk** of an egg, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.171, “PLit.Lond.” 170 (1st c.AD)}}; ἡ λευκότης γίγνεται πυρρή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 20}} of sediment in urine, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Epid.” 6.1.5.}}
**2** especially of persons **with red hair**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 16.2, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.108; π. τὸ Σκυθικὸν γένος 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 20; οἱ ἁλιεῖς π. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 966b26; π. τρίχες [prev. author] “GA” 785a19}}; of the colour of the first beard, γενειάς A.[same place]; γένυες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 32}}; [θρίξ] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.130, compare 6.3.}}
**3** generally, **tawny**, λέων {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “HF” [same place], 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 785b17, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.263 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); βοῦς “PLond.” 3.890.5, 10 (1st c.BC, written πυραί), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.363b; ἵππος NT.Rev.6.4; [πώλου] ἡ χρόα στίλβουσα τῆς χαίτης πυρσότατον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 22; αἶγες “PHib.” 1.120.6 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ χρώματα τῶν κυνῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 4.7.}}
**4** of more positive colour, **red**, χλανίς {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.139; ῥόδον 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.70; ἕψημα LXX.Gen.25.30}}; τὸ π. **redness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 329:}} —_Epic dialect_ _comparative_ (for πυρρότερα), πῠρώτερα φοινίσσεσθαι to be of a **brighter** red, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 798.}}
**5** **blushing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 900}}; also κύων.. πύρσ᾽ ἔχουσα δέργματα glaring with **bloodshot** eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1265.}}
**II** _paroxytone_ Πύρρος, ὁ, “Pyrrhus”, as _proper name_; also Πύρϝος, name of a horse, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“GDI” 3119h50 (from Corinth);compare Πυρϝίας “IG” 4.492.5 (Mycenae, 6th c.BC), Πυρϝαλίων [prev. work]517.4 (from Argos). (Prob. from *πυρσϝός.)}}

4451:
** πῠρ-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**firing, burning**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.9.1.}}
**2** **exposure to the action of fire**, as in cooking, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 928a24, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.7.2, “Lap.” 4, +others}}; ἡ ἐν τῷ ὑγρῷ π. **boiling**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 380b28}}; μαλακὴ π. Mnesith. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.357d.}}
**3** **proving by fire**, {{LXX+NT+5th c.AD+|LXX.Pro.27.21, NT.1Pet.4.12, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**4** **cautery**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.23.42.}}
**5** **destruction by fire**, γῆν πυρώσει ἀφανίζων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 1.11.4.}}
**II** **flame**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 369b6.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **burning desire**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 975.}}
**IV** **fever**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 2.240}}: _plural_, **feverish states**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 27.}}
**IV.2** **inflammation**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 60; στομάχου 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.124.}}

4452:
** πω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κω**, _enclitic_ Particle, **up to this time, yet**, in early _Epic dialect_ always with a _negative_, with which it sometimes forms one word, compare οὔπω, μήπω, etc.; sometimes a word is interposed, οὐδ᾽ ἄρα πώ τι ᾔδεε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.401; μὴ δή πω.. λυώμεθα.. ἵππους 23.7, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 27, 511, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 105, “Tr.” 591, 1061, etc.; μὴ συναλλάξαντά πω [prev. author] “OT” 1110.}}
**II** **at all**, with _negative_ in _Epic dialect_, οὐδέ τί πω ἴδμεν (που {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristophanes Byzantinus Philosophus) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.124; οὔ πω τλήσομ᾽ 3.306; μὴ δή πω χάζεσθε 15.426: after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus sometimes with questions which imply a negative, ἢ ξυναλλάξας τί π; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1130}} (variant που); πόλις ἀφισταμένη τίς πω τούτῳ ἐπεχείρησ; has **ever** a city meditating revolt..? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45.}}

4453:
** πωλ-έω**,
_Ionic dialectimperfect_ πωλέεσκε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 543, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.38}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ πωλησεῦντι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 12(1).3.2 (Rhodes, 1st c.AD)}}: _aorist_ ἐπώλησα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 16}}:—_passive_, _future_ in _middle_ form πωλήσεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 74.1: 2}} _future_ πεπωλήσεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 10.19}}: _aorist_ ἐπωλήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 260d, probably in “IG” 12.60.10}}:—**sell** or **offer for sale**, opposed to ὠνεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.165, 196, etc.}}; opposed to ἀποδίδοσθαι (of the actual sale), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.21, compare “Mem.” 2.5.5 (_passive_)}}; μετ᾽ ἀβακίου καὶ τραπεζίου π. ἑαυτόν **sell** oneself across the counter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 50: with _genitive_ pretii, ἐπώλεε ἐς Σάρδις χρημάτων μεγάλων}} **sold** at a high price for exportation to Sardis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.105, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus [same place]}}; πωλέω οὐδενὸς χρήματος refuse **to sell** it at any price, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.139; τὰ ξύμπαντα τούτου ἑνὸς ἂν πωλοῖτο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60; τῶν πόνων π. ἁμῖν πάντα τἀγάθ᾽ οἱ θεοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 287; ἀργυρίου π. τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.13, etc.}}; τὰ σφῶν αὐτῶν μικροῦ λήμματος π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 11.18}}; ἔρωμαι ὁπόσου πωλε; ask what he wants for it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.36; π. δὶς πρὸς ἀργύριον 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.6.4 (_passive_)}}; τὴν Ἀσίην πωλῶ πρὸς μύρα{{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.3}}; π. τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 13.1, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.13 (_passive_)}}; τι πρός τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.80, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 741b; ὑπὸ κήρυκος π. τὰ κοινά 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 51.22}}: _absolutely_, **carry on business, trade**, ἐν τῇ πόλει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 629.83 (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD)}}; π. πρός τινα **deal with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 722}}; π. πάλιν **retail**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 260d}}:— _passive_, **to be sold** or **offered for sale**, εἰν ἀγορῇ πωλεύμενα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 14.5, compare “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.160 (from Cyrene), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.105}}; of a person, **to be sold up,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1477.3 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**2** π. τέλη **let out** the taxes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.119; μέταλλα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 47.2; ὠνάς “SIG” 284.28 (Erythrae, 4th c.BC).}}
**2.b** **sell** or **farm out** offices, priesthoods, etc., [τὰς ἀρχάς] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 62.1; ἐπὶ τοῖσδε πωλοῦμεν τὴν ἱερωσύνην τοῦ Διονύσου “SIG” 1003.2 (Priene, 2nd c.BC).}}
**3** **sell**, i.e. **give up, betray**, τὰς γραφάς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.35; τὰ τῆς πόλεως [prev. author] 19.141; τὰ οἴκοι [prev. author] 7.17}}:—_passive_, of persons, **to be bought and sold, betrayed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 633.}}

4454:
** πῶλος**, ὁ
and ἡ, **foal**, whether **colt** or **filly**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.222 (_feminine_), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.246 (_masculine_); ἵππους.. πάσας θηλείας, πολλῇσι δὲ πῶλοι ὑπῆσαν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.681; ἐδάμασσε πώλους 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.8}}; νεοζυγὴς π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1010}}; κριθῶντα π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1641}}; ὁ ἔτι ἀδάμαστος π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.1}}; ἵπποι π., opposed to ἵπποι τέλειοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(1).12.18 (Ambryssus, 3rd c.AD): frequently used by Poets generally for ἵππος, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 326, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 313, 1062 (Lyric poetry), “El.” 705f: in races, πώλων ἀβόλων ἅρμα “IG” 22.2326.11; πώλοις τε ἀβόλοις καὶ τελείων τε καὶ ἀβόλων τοῖς μέσοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 834c: _metaphorically_, π. Κύπριδος, of courtesans, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 84.2.}}
**2** **any young animal**: of the elephant, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 610a33; camel, [prev. work] 630b34}}; κάμηλος π. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 768.2 (2nd c.AD)}}; of the dog, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.238 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus)}}; **ass,** {{NT|NT.Mark.11.2}}; ὄνοι π. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 8.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; **pullet**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 5.6; πῶλοι βουβαλίδων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.47.}}
**3** in Poets, in _feminine_, **young girl, maiden**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 75.1, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 142 (_anapaest meter_); πῶλον ἄζυγα λέκτρων [prev. author] “Hipp.” 546 (Lyric poetry), compare “fragment” 781.21 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 87, 4th c.BC: Epicrates Comicus 9; κακῆς γυναικὸς πῶλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 621}}: less frequently _masculine_, **young man**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 386 (_anapaest meter_), “Ph.” 947; ἀνδρὸς φίλου πῶλον.. ζυγέντ᾽ ἐν ἅρμασιν πημάτων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 794 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **a Corinthian coin**, from the figure of Pegasus upon it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 675, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.76.}}
**III** ἱερὸς π. Ἴσιδος, title of priest in Egypt, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 739.8 (2nd c.BC), “PGrenf.” 2.20.5 (2nd c.BC), “PRein.” 10.5, +others (2nd c.BC)}}; π. alone, of a priest of Demeter and Persephone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1444 (Messene, 4th-3rd c.BC).}}

4455:
** πώποτε**,
(πω, ποτέ) **ever yet**, in early _Epic dialect_ always with _negative_ and best written divisim, οὐ γάρ πώ ποτέ μ᾽ ὧδέ γ᾽ ἔρως φρένας ἀμφεκάλυψεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.442, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 650, etc.}} (referring to _future_, οὐκ ἄν πώ ποτ᾽ ἐγώ.. ἐλθοίμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 178); usually preceded by _negative_ in post- 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus writers, but πώ ποτ᾽ οὐδαμοῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1188: with}}_future_ in later Gr., κακία οὐχ εὑρεθήσεται ἐν σοὶ π{{LXX|. LXX.1Ki.25.28}}; οὐ μὴ διψήσει π. {{NT|NT.John.6.35, compare “PMag.Par.” 1.291}}; also οὐ μὴ γένωνται καθαραὶ π. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 78.27 (2nd c.BC)}}; μηδ᾽ ὄψον.. μετὰ τούτου π. δαίσῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Cratinus Junior Comicus 8}}: with _present_, {{Refs|“PMag.Leid.V.” 11.30}}; compare οὐ π., μὴ π., οὐδεπώποτε, μηδεπώποτε.
**II** sometimes, later, without a _negative_,
**II.1** with questions which imply a _negative_ (compare πω 11), ποῦ γὰρ π. ἄνευ νεφελῶν ὕοντ᾽ ἤδη τεθέασα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 370}}; ἤδή π. του ἤκουσα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 493d, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.7, etc.: with}}_future_, τίς γὰρ ἁλώσεται π.; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.45 (assuming variant).}}
**II.2** with a conditional clause, εἴ που ξένον τις ἠδίκησε πώποτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 147, compare “V.” 556, “Ach.” 405, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 196a, etc.}}
**II.3** after Relatives, οὕς φαμεν πώποτέ τι.. πρᾶξαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 352c; ἄλλον ὅστις πώποτέ τι γέγραφεν ἢ γράψει [prev. author] “Phdr.” 258d; ὅσοι ἐμοῦ π. ἀκηκόατε [prev. author] “Ap.” 19d, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.5, 4.50, +others}}
**II.4** with the _Article_ and Part., οἱ π. γενόμενοι who **ever yet** existed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.38, compare 16.33, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 116c, etc.}}: the Part. may be omitted, οἱ π. προδόται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 134}}; μεγίστους τῶν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.14, compare “PLips.” 119v. ii 4 (3rd c.AD).}}

4456:
** πωρ-όω**,
(from {πῶρος}) **petrify**, λίθος πεπωρωμένος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.13.}}
**II** **cause a stone** or **callus to form**:—_passive_, of **a stone forming** in the bladder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 9.}}
**II.2** **unite fractured bones by a callus**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Fract.” 47 (_passive_), 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.70,84}}:—_passive_, **become hard**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 802b8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.15.2}}; **become thickened, coagulated**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 222.}}
**III** in _passive_, **become insensible**, of the flesh, ὑπὸ τῆς πεπωρωμένης ἐκ τοῦ στέατος σαρκός {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Nymphis Historicus 16}}: _metaphorically_, **become insensible, obtuse**, or **blind**, of the heart, {{LXX+NT|NT.Mark.6.52, 8.17, NT.Rom.11.7; πεπώρωνται γὰρ ἀπὸ ὀργῆς οἱ ὀφθαλμοί μου LXX.Job.17.7.}}

4457:
** πώρ-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**process by which the extremities of fractured bones are reunited by a callus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 23 (_plural_), “Art.” 15, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.387.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **obtuseness, blindness**, τῆς καρδίας {{NT|NT.Mark.3.5, NT.Eph.4.18: _absolutely_, NT.Rom.11.25.}}

4458:
** πως**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κως**, _enclitic_ _adverb_ of Manner, **in any way, at all, by any means**, οὐ μέν π. ἅλιον πέλει ὅρκιον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.158, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.392}}; ἀλλὰ μὴ γένοιτό π. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1249}}; compare οὔπως, μήπως: frequently after other _adverbs_ of Manner, ὧδέ π. **somehow** so, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.7}}; ἄλλως π. **in some** other **way**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 3.1.20; τεχνικῶς π. [prev. work] 6.1.5}}; εὐσχημόνως π. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.3.9}}; sometimes merely to qualify their force, when it cannot be always rendered by any one English equivalent, ἀεί π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.211}}; μάλα π{{Refs 5th c.BC+|. 14.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.54}}; μόγις π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.: with Verbs, καὶ ἔτυχέ κως τοῦ μάγου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78, compare 150}}; τὸ γὰρ κάταγμα τυγχάνω ῥίψασά π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 695; ἠθάς εἰμί π. τῶν τῆσδε μύθων [prev. author] “El.” 372; πράσσοντές π. ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3}}; ἀπώκνησάν π. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.20; frequently after γάρ, ἔνεστι γάρ π... τῇ τυραννίδι νόσημα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 226, compare “Ch.” 958 (Lyric poetry), etc.: most frequently after hypothet. Particles, εἴ πως 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.460}}; ἐάν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1770 (_anapaest meter_), “Tr.” 584}}; ἤν π. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 399}}: expressing uncertainty, **I suppose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 3.108.}}
**II** πως, πῶς, or πώς, **in a certain way**, opposed to ἁπλῶς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1275a16; οὐδ᾽ ὁ ἁπλῶς ὀργιζόμενος, ἀλλ᾽ ὁ πῶς [prev. author] “EN” 1106a1; ἀλλὰ πῶς πραττόμενα καὶ πῶς νεμόμενα δίκαια [prev. work] 1137a12.}}
**II.2** πὼς μὲν.., πὼς δὲ.. **in one way.., in another.. **, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de communi mathematica scientia” 13, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista in “de An.” 4.25-28, +others}}; πῶς μὲν.., ὅλως δὲ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1263a26. (πῶς is}} _adverb_ of stem πο-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **q[uglide]o-**), whence ποῦ, ποῖ, πῇ, etc.)

4459:
** πῶς**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κῶς**, _interrogative_ _adverb_ of Manner, **how?** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.123, etc.}}; sometimes to express displeasure, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.26, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 391, “Ph.” 1031, “Tr.” 192}}; to express astonishment or doubt, π. εἶπα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 798, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 407, etc.}}; π. λέγει; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1407}}; π. φῄ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 268, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 575}}; π. τοῦτ᾽ ἔλεξα; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 793}}; π. τοῦτ᾽ εἶπες α; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 309c}}; also π. μὴ φῶμεν..; surely we must, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 161e.}}
**b** in dialogue, to ask explanation, with a repetition of a word used by the previous speaker, δίκαια— Answ. π. δίκαι; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 832}}; μὴ δίκαιος ὤν—Answ. π. μὴ δίκαιο; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 412}}; συμβολάς—Answ. π. συμβολά; {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 143}}; πῶς alone, **how so**, π., ὅς γε..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1386.}}
**2** with a second _interrogative_ in the same clause, π. ἐκ τίνος νεώς ποτε.. ἥκετ; **how** and by what ship..? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1543; τί τἀμὰ—π. ἔχει—θεσπίσματ; [prev. work] 873}}; π. τί τοῦτο λέγει; **how** say you and what? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 22b, compare “Tht.” 146d, 208e, etc.}}
**3** with _genitive_ modi, π. ἀγῶνος ἥκομε; **how** are we come off in it? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 751; π. ἔχει πλήθους ἐπισκοπεῖ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 451c.}}
**4** with Verbs of selling, **how? at what price?** π. ὁ σῖτος ὤνιο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 758, compare “Eq.” 480}}; τὰ δ᾽ ἄλφιθ᾽ ὑμῖν π. ἐπώλου;—Answ. τεττάρων δραχμῶν.. τὸν κόφινον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 13.}}
**II** with other Particles, π. ἂν..; π. κε (ν)..; **how possibly..?** π. ἂν ἔπειτ᾽ ἀπὸ σεῖο.. λιποίμην οἶο; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.437, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.65, etc.}}; π. ἂν γένοιτ᾽ ἂν.. ποδῶν ἔκμακτρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 534}}: so with _indicative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.202, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 96 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**II.b** in Trag., π. ἄν with _optative_ is frequently used to express a wish, **O how** might it be? i.e. **would that** it might..! π. ἂν θάνοιμ; π. ἂν ὀλοίμη; etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 388 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 796 (Lyric poetry), compare “Hipp.” 208 (_anapaest meter_), 345}}; rare in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 22}} (a trace of this usage appears in {{LXX+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.195): in later Prose, π. ἂν γένοιτο ἑσπέρ; LXX.Deu.28.67}}; also with _aorist_ _subjunctive_ (without ἄν), π. κοιμηθ; {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 9.40}}; π. μή μοι μέγας λέων ἐπιφαν; {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.10.10}}; with _present_, π. μὴ χρῄζ; M.Ant. [prev. cited]
**II.2** π. ἄρα..; in reply, **how then?** π. τ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἴω..; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.188, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.22, “hymnus ad Apollinem” 19, 207.}}
**II.3** π. γὰρ..; also in reply, as if something had gone before, [**that cannot be**], **for how can..?** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.123, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.337, etc.}}; π. γὰρ κάτοιδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 250, compare 1383; see below 111.1.}}
**II.4** π. δὲ..; to introduce a strong objection, π. δὲ σὺ νῦν μέμονας, κύον ἀδδεές..; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.481, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.31}}; δόξει δὲ π.; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 261.}}
**II.5** π. δ; **how in the world?** π. δὴ φῂς πολέμοιο μεθιέμε; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.351, compare 18.364, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 543, etc.}}; also π. γὰρ δή..; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.70}}; π. δῆτα..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 622, 1211, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 79, etc.}}
**II.6** π. καὶ..; **just how..?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 515, “Ph.” 1354, etc.}}; π. δὲ καὶ..; [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 721, see at {καί} 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 6}}]; but καὶ π...; to introduce an objection, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1348, see at {καί} 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.2: hence καὶ π.}}]; alone, **but how? impossible!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.134c, “Tht.” 163d, etc.}}
**II.7** π. οὐ..; **how not so..?** i.e. **surely it is so.. **, π. οὐ δεινὰ εἴργασθ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.66, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 398, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.273.}}
**II.8** π. οὖν..; like{π. ἄρα.}.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 297, 340, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 568, etc.}}; π. ἂν οὖν.. with _optative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 243, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 98.}}
**II.9** π. ποτε..; **how ever..?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1210 (Lyric poetry), “Ph.” 687 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** πῶς followed by several of the abovenamed Particles is frequently used in elliptical sentences, as,
**III.1** π. γά; inserted parenthesis in a negative sentence, **for how is it possible? how can** or **could it be?** hence in emphatic denial, κἀγὼ μὲν οὐκ ἔδρασα, τοῦτ᾽ ἐπίσταμαι, οὐδ᾽ αὖ σύ· π. γά; {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 911}}; οὐκ ἀπορῶν (π. γά;), ὅς γε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.312, compare 21.217, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 263c, etc.}}; οὐδ᾽ ἐπὶ τὴν ἑστίαν καταφυγών (π. γὰρ ἄ;), ὅστις.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.27}}; π. γὰρ ο; **how can it but be?** i.e. **it must be so**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 754, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1307, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 160c, +others}}; π. γά; (i.e. ἄλλως ἔχει) is so used in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 279.}}
**III.2** π. δ; **how so?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 601, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 664, 673, etc.}}; π. δῆτ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 469b}}; π. δα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1212.}}
**III.3** π. δ᾽ ο; like{π. γὰρ οὔ}; (see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|above 1), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153b, “R.” 457a}}; π. δ᾽ οὐχ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1015, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1027; parenthetically, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OT” 567}}; π. δ᾽ οὐκ ἄν..; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 759.}}
**III.4** π. οὖ; **how** then? **how** next? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1376, “Hipp.” 598, 1261, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.124}}; π. οὖν ἄν..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.64.}}
**III.5** π. δοκεῖ; parenthesis, in conversation, **how** think you? hence (losing all _interrogative_ force),= λίαν, **wonderfully**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 742, “Nu.” 881, “Ach.” 24; also π. οἴει σφόδρα [prev. author] “Ra.” 54; compare δοκέω 1.2.}}
**IV** π. in indirect questions for ὅπως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 677, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 991 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 614, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.36, etc.; ἐθαύμαζον ἂν π... ἔδεισαν “IG” 12(3).174.28 (Cnidus, Epist.Aug.); ζητηθήσεται π. ὅτι καὶ τοῦτο ἀληθές ἐστι 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.16.}}
**V** in exclamations, ὦ π. πονηρόν ἐστιν ἀνθρώπου φύσις τὸ σύνολον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 2}}; π. παραχρῆμα ἐξηράνθη.. ! {{NT|NT.Matt.21.20}}; π. δυσκόλως.. ! {{NT|NT.Mark.10.23.}}

4464:
** ῥάβδος**, ἡ,
**rod, wand**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), etc.}}; lighter than the βακτηρία or walking-stick, [{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.4 (but ={βακτηρία}, NT.Matt.10.10, +others). —Special uses:}}]
**1** **magic wand**, as that of Circe, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.238, 319, etc.}}; that with which Athena touched Odysseus, to restore his youthful appearance, χρυσείῃ ῥάβδῳ ἐπεμάσσατο {{Refs|16.172}}; that with which Hermes overpowers the senses of man, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.343}}; that with which Hades rules the ghosts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.33}}; **divining-rod**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.67.}}
**2** **fishing-rod**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.251.}}
**3** **limed twig**, for catching small birds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 527.}}
**4** **shaft** of a hunting-spear, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.3, 16.}}
**5** **staff of office**, like the earlier σκῆπτρον, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 367a, LXX.Psa.44(45).7, 109(110).2}}; carried by a βραβευτής, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.25J.:— uncertain in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 248 for ῥαβδοῦχος.}}
**6** **wand borne by the** ῥαψῳδός, τὸν ἐπὶ ῥάβδῳ μῦθον ὑφαινόμενον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 138 (= “fragment” 3.10P.), compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.30.3: hence κατὰ ῥάβδον ἐπέων}} according to the **measure** of his (Homer's) verses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).38(56).}}
**7** **rod for chastisement**, ῥ. κοσμοῦσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 700c}}; μάστιξ ἢ ῥ. **riding-switch**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 8.4}}; ῥ. βοηλάτις ox-**goad,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca Appx. 4.200 (2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus); ξαίνεσθαι ῥάβδοις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 51, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 11.153 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}: of the **fasces** of the Roman lictors, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.29.6, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.11, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.2.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 10, “Luc.” 36}}; πρὸς πέντε ῥάβδους, = Latin _at_ (i.e. **ad) quinque fasces (CIL**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|8.7044 (from Numidia)), “OGI” 543.18 (Ancyra, 2nd c.AD), “IGRom.” 3.175 ([prev. passage], 2nd c.AD)}}; compare ῥαβδονόμος, ῥαβδοῦχος.
**8** **shepherd's staff** or **crook**, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.22(23).4, LXX.Mic.7.14.}}
**9** ῥ. κληρονομίας **measuring-rod**,{{LXX|LXX.Psa.73(74).2.}}
**10** **stitch**, ἔντοσθεν δὲ βοείας ῥάψε θαμειὰς χρυσείῃς ῥάβδοισι διηνεκέσιν περὶ κύκλον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.297}} (unless it means **rivets, studs**).
**II** **young shoot** of some trees,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|Ion Trag. 40, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.1.2.}}
**III** **streak** or **stripe** on the skin of animals, διαποίκιλα ῥάβδοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 525a12}}; of fish, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 73}}; of clothes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.53}}; **fluting** of a column, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 4.448.7 (Didyma, 2nd c.BC, _plural_)}}; of minerals, **vein**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 4.12.6, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.37}}; **streak** or **shaft** of light, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 377a30, “Mu.” 395a31, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 11.}}
**IV** in Grammars,
**IV.1** **line, verse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4.63.}}
**IV.2** **a critical mark**, like{ὀβελός}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**IV.3** **stroke** forming a letter, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theodectes Tragicus 6.6.}}

4465:
** ῥάβδουχ-ος**
(_properispomenon_), ὁ, **one who carries a rod** or **staff of office**:
**1** **judge, umpire at a contest**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 338a.}}
**2** **magistrate's attendant, staff-bearer, beadle**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 734, “UPZ” 3.6 (2nd c.BC, probably), “IG” 9(2).735 (Larissa, 3rd c.BC), 1109.24 (Coropa, 2nd c.BC), NT.Act.16.35; so, probably, in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.50}}: especially at Rome, of the **lictors who carried the fasces**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.26.10, etc.: also ῥαβδοῦχοι, αἱ}}, **female attendants** on Oenanthe, mother of Agathocles, {{Refs|[prev. author] 15.29.13.}}

4468:
** ῥᾳδιουργ-ία**, ἡ,
**self-indulgence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.34 (probably the interpretation εὐκολία in 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, etc., refers to this passage).}}
**2** **laziness, sloth**,{{Refs|[prev. work] 7.5.74, “Mem.” 2.1.20.}}
**II** **knavery,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PEnteux. 30.11 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.9.5, 13.4.4}}; δόλος καὶ ῥ. {{NT|NT.Act.13.10, etc.}}; of historians, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.25E. 2}}; **fraud**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 16.}}

4470:
** ῥάκος** [ᾰ], εος, τό,
**ragged, tattered garment**, δὸς ῥ. ἀμφιβαλέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.178, compare 13.434, 14.342, 349 (never in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”)}}; ἀνθ᾽ ἱματίου μὲν ἔχειν ῥ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 540; ῥ. φορεῖ 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 204.6, “PPetr.” 3p.115 (3rd c.BC),compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 146:}} frequently in _plural_ ῥάκεα, _Attic dialect_ ῥάκη, **rags, tatters**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.512, 18.67, 74, 19.507, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 39, 274; ἐν ῥάκεσι περιφθείρεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 9.10.}}
**2** generally, **strip of cloth**, ῥάκεα φοινίκεα {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.76, cf. NT.Matt.9.16, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 35.3}}: even a **strip** of flesh, σώματος ῥ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1023.}}
**3** collectively, **rag, lint**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.36; ῥάκη λινᾶ 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.75.15.}}
**II** in _plural_ also, **rents in the face, wrinkles**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1065.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **rag, remnant**, εἰκάσαι τὸ ἐρείπιον ῥάκει οἰκίας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Anonymus cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1413a6}}; of an old seaman, ἁλίοιο βίου ῥ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.242 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus), compare 7.380 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 32.}} —The _Aeolic dialect_ form βράκος (which see), used of a garment, lacks the sense 'ragged'.

4472:
** ῥαν-τίζω**,
={ῥαίνω}, {{NT|NT.Heb.9.13, 19, 21}}:—_passive_, **to be sprinkled**, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Lev.6.27, +others, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.521a, 6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus “de febribus” 4.}}
**II** of the effect, **purify**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.50(51).7; ἐρραντισμένοι τὰς καρδίας ἀπὸ συνειδήσεως πονηρᾶς NT.Heb.10.22.}}
**III** ῥαντίζει: σκώπτει, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

4474:
** ῥᾰπίζω**,
(ῥαπίς) **strike with a stick, cudgel, thrash**, τινα {{LXX+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 7.4, 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 64 (_passive_), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.35, 223, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.57, LXX.Judg.16.25, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.6.6, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.40 W.; τινὰ ῥάβδῳ “Anacreontea” 29.2:—}}_passive_, ῥ. ἐκ τῶν ἀγώνων **to be flogged** off the course, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 42, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.59}}: _Ionic dialect_ _perfect_ _participle_, ῥεραπισμένα νῶτα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 166.}}
**II** **slap in the face** (later for _Attic dialect_ ἐπὶ κόρρης πατάξαι), ἐπὶ κόρρης ῥ. (_metaphorically_) {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.713c; κατὰ κόρρης 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 2.24; εἰς τὴν σιαγόνα NT.Matt.5.39}}:—_passive_, ῥαπισθῆναί τε καὶ πληγὰς λαβεῖν ἁπαλαῖσι χερσίν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 22.5; ἐρραπίσθη τὴν γνάθον 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 97, compare “Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 300}}; ῥαπίζειν distinguished from κολαφίζειν, {{NT|NT.Matt.26.67.}}
**III** generally, **strike, beat**, [τὸν ἀέρα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 419b23}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mete.” 368a16, 370a14, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 398.}}

4475:
** ῥάπ-ισμα** [ᾰ], ατος, τό,
**stroke**, ἀνθράκων ῥαπίσμασι (Abresch ῥιπ-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 217.21.}}
**2** **slap on the face,** {{NT|NT.Mark.14.65, NT.John.18.22}}; in Latin form **rhapismata,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th-6th c.AD: Codex Justinianus 8.48.6; ῥ. λαμβάνειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 8.2; ῥ. ἀμφὶ πρόσωπα “Anthologia Graeca” 5.288 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** **weal,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Arch.Papyrus 3. 418.30 (6th c.AD).}}

4476:
** ῥᾰφίς**,
_Doric dialect_ **ῥᾰπίς** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 51)}}, ίδος, ἡ, (ῥάπτω) **needle**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.66 (where 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.134 read ῥαφίῳ, +others γραφίσι), 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 38, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 61.14, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 109.1 (ῥανίδα codices), “Anthologia Graeca” 11.110 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus), “Hermes” 38.283}}; διὰ τρυπήματος (variant{τρήματος}) ῥαφίδος διελθεῖν (variant{εἰσελθεῖν}) {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.19.24:—in _Attic dialect_ replaced by βελόνη, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 72.}}
**II** **garfish**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Belone acus, Epicharmus Comicus [same place], 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 294, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.172, “C.” 2.392.}}

4482:
** ῥέω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.149, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ **ῥείω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 263 (uncertain), 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 1074, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.36 (1st c.BC: Erycius Epigrammaticus), but not in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}: _imperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔρρει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.86, 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 1.4, but elsewhere in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus ἔρρεε or ῥέε}}: _future_ ῥεύσομαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 448, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 384, 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 15.4, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 130.5, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ αἱμορροΐδων” 5}}; also ῥευσοῦμαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 356a16, 361a33}}; later ῥεύσω, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.124 (1st c.AD: Bassus, Lollius Epigrammaticus)}}: _aorist_ ἔρρευσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 526 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 11, “Int.” 23, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.33, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.32 (Parmenio Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.15.7}} (ἀπ-), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.7.4, etc.}}:—but the _Attic dialect_ _future_ and _aorist_ are of _passive_ form, ῥῠήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.140, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος ἀνθρώπου” 5}}; ἐρρύην [ῠ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.116, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.30, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 84c, etc., as also in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.138}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐξ-ερρύα, see at {ἐκρέω}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ ἐ[γ]ρυᾷ {{Refs|“GDI” 3591a51 (from Calymna)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ῥύη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.455}}: _perfect_ ἐρρύηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 485d, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.5}}; later ἔρρυκα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.398.}}—A _present_ _middle_ **ῥέομαι** occurs also in Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.140 (see. below), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 3, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 71, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.25.9, etc.; so ἐρρεῖτο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1602, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.31, etc.}}—This Verb does not _contraction_ εη, εο, εω:—**flow, run, stream, gush**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.204, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.300, 17.86, etc.: with _dative_ of that which flows, [πηγὴ] ὕδατι ῥέει}} the fountain **runs with** water,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.149, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.70, “IG” 12.54.7; ῥέε δ᾽ αἵματι γαῖα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.65, etc.; φάραγγες ὕδατι.. ῥέουσαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 449 (troch.); ῥεῖ γάλακτι πέδον ῥεῖ δ᾽ οἴνῳ [prev. author] “Ba.” 142 (Lyric poetry)}}; οἴνῳ.. ἔρρει χαράδρα Telecl.[same place] (see. entry near the end); (also in _middle_, ἱδρῶτι ῥεούμενοι (for the sake of meter for ῥεόμενοι, compare μαχεούμενοι) Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.140; φόνῳ ναῦς ἐρρεῖτο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1602); πόλιν χρυσῷ ῥέουσαν [prev. author] “Tr.” 995: so _metaphorically_, πολλῷ ῥ. ἐπαίνῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 527}}: rarely with _accusative_ in the same sense (see. below {{Refs|11.2)}}: also with _genitive_, ἀσφάλτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.5.8; πολλοῦ ὕδατος 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.9.4: sometimes with _nominative_, Ζεὺς χρυσὸς ῥυείς 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.59, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 5.32 (Parmenio Epigrammaticus).}}
**b** the post-{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} expression for **a full stream** is μέγας ῥεῖ, ῥέουσι μεγάλοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.25; μέγας ἐρρύη [prev. author] 8.138, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5; ῥ. οὐδὲν ἧσσον ἢ νῦν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.129}}; also πολὺς ῥεῖ, _metaphorically_ of men, ῥεῖ πολὺς ὅδε λεώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 80 (Lyric poetry); Κύπρις ἢν πολλὴ ῥυῇ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 443 (compare below 2); so ῥ. μου τὸ δάκρυον πολύ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1034}}; also ἐς ἔρωτα ἅπας ῥ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 193; πρὸς τὸν Ἀλκιβιάδην ὁ δῆμος ὅλος ἐρρύη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 21.}}
**c** of a river, also ῥ. ἀπὸ τηκομένης χιόνος **derive its stream** from melted snow, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.22.}}
**d** proverbial, ἄνω ῥεῖν **flow** upwards, of inversion of the usual or right order, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 520; ἄνω ποταμῶν ἐρρύησαν οἱ.. λόγοι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.287}}; compare ἄνω (B)1.
**e** ταῦτα μὲν ῥείτω κατ᾽ οὖρον (see. οὖρος (A)){{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 468.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ of things, ἐκ χειρῶν βέλεα ῥέον from their hands **rained** darts, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.159; ῥεῖ μάλιστα ὁ ἀὴρ ῥέων ἐν τοῖς ὑψηλοῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 347a34, compare 349a34; φλὸξ ῥυεῖσα 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Brutus” 31; so τὴν Αἴτνην ῥυῆναι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 2}}; especially of a **flow** of words, ἀπὸ γλώσσης μέλιτος γλυκίων ῥέεν αὐδή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.249, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 39.97; ἔπε᾽ ἐκ στόματος ῥεῖ μείλιχα [prev. work] 84}}: _absolutely_, of the tongue, **run glibly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 557; so θρασυνομένῳ καὶ πολλῷ ῥέοντι καθ᾽ ὑμῶν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.136}}: hence, of words or sentiments, **to be current**, κληδόνος καλῆς μάτην ῥεούσης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 259.}}
**3** **fall, drop off**, e.g. of hair, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.393, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 29, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.89, etc.}}; of ripe fruit, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.4.14, “Geoponica” 9.12}}; of over-ripe corn, ἤδη ῥέοντα τὸν στάχυν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 88.14}}; **wear out**, εἰ ῥέοι τὸ σῶμα καὶ ἀπολλύοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 87d}}; of a house, **to be in a tumble-down condition**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.51.28, 3rd c.BC: Teles Philosophus p.27 H.}}; ῥέουσαν σύγκρισιν στῆσαι to stay a **collapse** of the system, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 5.27.1.}}
**4** of molten objects, **liquefy, run**, ῥεῖ πᾶν ἄδηλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 698; τήκεται ὁ λίθος.. ὥστε καὶ ῥεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 383b6, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 9.}}
**5** **to be in perpetual flux and change**, ἅπανθ᾽ ὁρῶ ἅμα τῇ τύχῃ ῥέοντα μεταπίπτοντά τε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 200; ὡς ἰόντων ἁπάντων ἀεὶ καὶ ῥεόντων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 439c, compare 411c; κινεῖται καὶ ῥεῖ.. τὰ πάντα [prev. author] “Tht.” 182c: hence οἱ ῥέοντες, of the Heraclitean philosophers, opposed to οἱ τοῦ ὅλου στασιῶται, [prev. work] 181a.}}
**5.b** '**run**', of ink, etc., _metaphorically_, στιγμῆς ῥυείσης γραμμὴν φαντασιούμεθα.., γραμμῆς δὲ ῥυείσης πλάτος ἐποιήσαμεν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.99; compare ῥυίσκομαι 11.}}
**6** of persons, ῥ. ἐπί τι **to be inclined, given** to a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.5; πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 485d; οἱ ταύτῃ ῥυέντες [prev. work] 495b.}}
**7** **leak**, of a ship, opposed to στεγανὸν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 554, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.50.7}}; λύχνοι ῥέοντες {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|probably in Roussel “Cultes Egyptiens” p.222 (Delos, 2nd c.BC)}}; of a roof, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 248; [ἀγγεῖον] ῥέον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.782e; οἰνοχόαι ῥέουσαι “Michel” 815.131 (Delos, 4th c.BC).}}
**8** **to have a flux**, τὰς κοιλίας τὰς ῥεούσας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.41.}}
**9** _impersonal_, ἐκ ῥινῶν ἐρρύη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.19.}}
**II** very rarely _transitive_, **let flow, pour**, ἔρρει χοάς [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 528 (as variant for{αἴρει}):}}] —this differs from the usage
**II.2** with _accusative_ _cognate_, ῥείτω γάλα, μέλι, **let** the land **run** milk, honey, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.124, 126}}; αἷμα ῥυήσεται, of the Nile, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 133; οἶνον ῥέων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.7, cf. LXX.Joel.3(4).18, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 287:—in place of this _accusative_ the best writers commonly used the _dative_, see above 1.1.}}
**III** see at {ῥέον}. (Cf. ῥόϝος, Sanskrit _sravati_, Lithuanian _sravēti_ 'flow': I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **srèw-** alternating with **sr[ocaron]w-** and **sr[ucaron]-.**)

4485:
** ῥῆγ-μα**, ατος, τό,
(ῥήγνυμι) **breakage, fracture**, joined with σπάσμα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 4,compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.198, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.74}}; with στρέμμα (a strain), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.21, 11.14.}}
**2** **laceration, rupture**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.160, 18(2).882, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 635a4.}}
**3** **rent, tear**, in clothes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 38.}}
**4** **cleft, chasm**, ῥ. τῆς γῆς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 628b29}}; **chink**, ἐν τοίχοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.6.8}}; **breach** in a dyke, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.131r. 45, 60 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** **lesion** or **rupture** of tissue, ὅταν ὑπὸ βίης διαστέωσιν αἱ σάρκες ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 11, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.238, 10.232; especially oflung, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 14, “Morb.” 1.20}}:hence **ῥηγμᾰτίας**, ου, ὁ, **one who has such a rupture**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Aër.” 4, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.146, 4.10; τοὺς ἐκ βηχὸς ῥηγματίας “Hippiatrica” 22}}; but ῥηγματίας πλεύμονος perhaps = **pleurisy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.53}}; **ῥηγμᾰτώδης**, ες, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Epid.” 7.26.}}

4486:
** ῥήγνῡμι**
or **ῥηγνύω**
**A** (ἀναρ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φυσῶν” 10}}: _imperfect_ κατ-ερρήγνυε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.63, etc.)}}; later **ῥήσσω**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.640, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 93, “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”; ῥήσσεσθαι “PHolm.” 6.3, compare 4.22}}; ἀπο-, δια-ρρήσσεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 17, 42}}; **ῥήττω**, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 11.14.8, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.150 (variant{ῥήσσει}), (περι-) [prev. author] 2.98, 3.18 (variant{περιρρήσσει}); ῥήττεσθαι 3rd-2nd c.BC: Bito Mechanicus 45.8, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.3.18}}]: _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ ῥήγνυσκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.141}}: _future_ ῥήξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|12.262, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2, (ἐκ-) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 775}}: _aorist_ ἔρρηξα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.348, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 960; ῥῆξα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.6:}}_perfect_ ἔρρηχα (δι-) {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.14.30, 15.32:—}}_middle_ ῥήγνῠμαι, _future_ ῥήξομαι, _aorist_ ἐρρηξάμην, all in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” (12.257, 224, 291)}}, _present_ also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 4, 12}}: _aorist_ ἐρρηξάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 835, (κατ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.13}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ῥήξαντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.90}}:—_passive_, _subjunctive_ ῥήγνῡται {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 19.4}}: _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperfect_ ῥηγνύατο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 817}}: _future_ ῥᾰγήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.668a, (διαρ-, ἐκ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 340, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 369, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἐρράγην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 583, etc.}}; later ἐρρήχθην, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd-4th c.AD: Tryphiodorus Epicus 11}}; δια-ρρηχθῇ (variant{-ρραγῇ}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 29:}}_perfect_ ἔρρηγμαι (συν-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.137}}; but _intransitive_ _perfect_ ἔρρωγα is more frequently, see below with {{Refs|1}}; _perfect_ _participle_ _feminine_ ἐρρηγεῖα, see below with {{Refs|2}}; _masculine_ _plural_ κατ-ερρηγότας {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}} The word is hardly used by correct _Attic dialect_ Prose-writers, except in _passive_:—**break asunder, rend, shatter**, τεῖχος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.198; πύλας 13.124; σάκος 21.165; θώρηκας 2.544; ἱμάντα 3.375; νευρήν 8.328; ὀστέον 20.399; χρόα 23.673}}; only once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, προτόνους ἔρρηξ᾽ ἀνέμοιο θύελλα 12.409}}:—later, especially **rend** garments, in sign of grief, ῥ. πέπλους {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 199, 468}}; ῥ. ἕλκεα **make grievous** wounds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.29}}; ῥ. ὀστᾶ, σάρκας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 994, “Ba.” 1130; ἀρότροις γῆς δάπεδον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 515}}: in _Ionic dialect_ and later Prose, ῥήγνυσι.. τὸν ἀμφὶ τὴν ὄψιν χιτῶνα {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19; ῥήττειν νευράν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.57; τὰ δεσμά 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 17.1; τὰς πύλας [prev. author] “Par.” 46; μὴ στραφέντες ῥήξωσιν ὑμᾶς NT.Matt.7.6}}:—_middle_, **break for oneself, get broken**, ὄρνυσθ᾽.. ῥήγνυσθε δὲ τεῖχος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.440, compare 224, 257, 291}}:—_passive_, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**A.2** **break** a line of battle or body of men, ῥ. φάλαγγα, ὅμιλον, στίχας ἀνδρῶν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.6, 11.538, 15.615}}; τὸ μέσον ῥῆξαι **break through** the centre, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.113: _absolutely_, ἐρρηξάτην ἐς κύκλα.. ὅπλων}} **broke through**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 210.9:}} — _middle_, ῥήξασθαι φάλαγγας, στίχας, **break oneself a way through** the lines, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.90, 13.680, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 835; ῥηξαμένῳ θέσθαι παρὰ νηυσὶ κέλευθον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.411.}}
**A.3** **let break loose**, ἔριδα ῥ. {{Refs|20.55 (_middle_).}}
**A.4** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ῥῆξαι φωνήν}} **let loose** the voice, of children and persons who have been dumb or silent, **break into** speech, **speak out**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.85, 2.2, 5.93, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 357, 960; ῥῆξαι αὐδήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 710}}; later ῥήξασθαι φωνήν, θρόον αὐδῆς, φθόγγον, **utter,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 5.221 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus), 7.597 (4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator), 9.61: _absolutely_, ῥῆξον καὶ βόησον}} **cry aloud**, {{LXX|LXX.Isa.54.1}}; see belowwith
**A.5** also δακρύων ῥήξασα.. νάματα **having let loose, having burst into** floods of tears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 919}}; κλαυθμὸν ῥ. {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 36; ῥ. τὰ ὄρη εὐφροσύνην LXX.Isa.49.13; ῥήγνυσι πηγὰς ὁ χῶρος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 19; ῥ. νεφέλην ἔς τινας 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.27}}; see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**B** _passive_, **break, break asunder, burst**, κῦμα ῥήγνυτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.67; κῦμα.. χέρσῳ ῥηγνύμενον 4.425, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 377}}; of clouds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 378}}; ῥαγῆναί τι τῆς γῆς, as in an earthquake, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 359d; ῥαγεῖσα Θηβαίων κόνις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 958; ἱμάτια ῥαγέντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.16; ὀστέον ῥήγνυται τιτρωσκόμενον 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 4}}; ῥήττονται ὑδρίαι (by the cold) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.3.18}}; τοῖς βασκάνοις εἶναι ῥήγνυσθαι may the envious **burst**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 50 (26). 69; τοῖς εἴ τις εὐδοκιμήσειεν ἐπί τῳ ῥηγνυμένοις 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 29.13, compare “Or.” 1.207.}}
**B.2** **burst forth**, like lightning, βροντὴ δ᾽ ἐρράγη δι᾽ ἀστραπῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 583, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.919b; so καταμηνίων ῥαγέντων 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 5.32, compare “Nat.Mul.” 13, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 582b10, etc.}}
**B.3** of ships, **to be wrecked**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.21: _metaphorically_, πολλῶν ῥαγεισῶν ἐλπίδων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 505.}}
**B.4** of a stone, γράμματι ῥηγνύμενον **scored** with lettering, i.e. inscribed, Puchstein {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“epigram Gr.” p.76 (Memphis, 2nd c.BC).}}
**C** _intransitive_, like _passive_, **break** or **burst forth**, ἔρρηξεν ἔμετος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.24; τὸ πνεῦμα ῥήγνυσι [prev. author] “Nat.Puer.” 12; εἰ ἐθελήσει ῥήξας ὑπερβῆναι ὁ ποταμός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.99: _metaphorically_, ὁποῖα χρῄζει ῥηγνύτω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1076 (in answer to the words δέδοιχ᾽ ὅπως μὴ.. ἀναρρήξει κακά): frequently in this signification in}}_perfect_ ἔρρωγα, **to have broken out**, ἔρρωγε παγὰ δακρύων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 852 (Lyric poetry): _metaphorically_, κακῶν πέλαγος ἔρρωγεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 433; τάδ᾽ ἐκ δυοῖν ἔρρωγεν.. κακά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1280; σοὶ τάδ᾽ ἔρρωγεν κακά 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1338; ἐρρωγότες λόγοι}}**broken, disjointed,** {{Refs|Comica Adespota 661.}}
**C.2** in literal sense, γῆ ἐρρηγεῖα (-υῖα) **broken, arable**, opposed to ἄρρηκτος, {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.18, +others (ϝρηγ- (compare _Aeolic dialect_ _aorist_ _passive_ εὐράγη Hdn.Gr.2.640, ϝρῆξις, αὔρηκτος)}}, _cognate_ with Lithuanian _rēžti_ 'cut, notch, furrow', **rúožas** 'stripe, streak, strip'.)

4487:
** ῥῆμα**, ατος, τό,
(ἐρῶ) **that which is said** or **spoken, word, saying**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 50, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1152, 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.14, 92}} (where perhaps it ={ῥήτρα}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|11.2), 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. below), etc.; in Prose first in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (assuming variant), ὁ νόος τοῦ ῥ. 7.162; τὰ λεγόμενά τινων [ῥήματα] 8.83}}; τοῦ Πιττακοῦ.. περιεφέρετο τοῦτο τὸ ῥ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 343b}}; τὸ δόγμα τε καὶ ῥ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 464a}}; opposed to ἔργματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.6}}; opposed to ἔργον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.111}}; opposed to τὸ ἀληθές, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 102b: proverbial, ῥήματα ἀντ᾽ ἀλφίτων}} 'fine words butter no parsnips', cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus; ῥήματα πλέκων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.94}}; ῥήματα θηρεύειν catch at one's **words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.9}}; ῥ. ἱπποβάμονα, ῥ. μυριάμφορον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 821, “Pax” 521}}; ῥήματος ἐχόμενον depending on the **word**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 656c}}; τῷ ῥ. τῷ τόδε προσχρώμενοι **the word** τόδε, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 49e}}; τῷ ῥ. λέγειν, εἰπεῖν, say **in so many words**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 340d, “Grg.” 450e, compare “Tht.” 166d}}; κατὰ ῥῆμα ἀπαγγεῖλαι **word for word**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.122.}}
**2** **phrase**, opposed to ὄνομα (a single word), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399b, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.72; λέγοντες ἐν μύθοις τε καὶ ἐν ῥήμασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 840c.}}
**2.b** **verse, line**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1379, compare 97.}}
**3** **subject of speech, matter**, Hebraism in {{LXX+NT|LXX and NT, LXX.Gen.15.1, 22.1, LXX.Deu.2.7, NT.Luke.1.37, 65, 2.15; compare ῥητός IV. 2.}}
**II** Grammars, **verb**, opposed to ὄνομα (noun), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 262af, “Cra.” 425a, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1457a14, 2nd c.BC: Diogenes Babylonius Stoicus 3.213}}:— from the fact that a Verb usually forms **the predicate** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Interpretatione” 16b6), ῥῆμα}} is applied to an _adjective_ **when used as a predicate**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 16a13, 20b1.}}

4489:
** ῥήτωρ**, ορος, ὁ,
also ἡ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 945 (compare “Th.” 292)}}: (ἐρῶ):— **public speaker**, μύθων ῥήτορες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 124 (_anapaest meter_), compare “fragment” 597.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.129, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 149b25, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.272S., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.131a, etc.}}; especially at Athens, οἱ ῥήτορες the **public speakers in the** ἐκκλησία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 38, 680, “Eq.” 60, 358, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.1, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.22, etc.; _sing._ probably in “IG” 12.45.21}}; οἱ δέκα ῥ. the Ten Attic Orators, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 29}}; ὁ ῥ. 'par excellence' = Demosthenes, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ εὑρέσεως” 4.1, +others}}
**2** **one who gives sentence, judge**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1090.}}
**3** **advocate,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 37.4 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**4** later, **teacher of eloquence, rhetorician**, {{Refs|“OGI” 712 (from Egypt), etc.}}
**II** as _adjective_, ῥ. λόγος **oratory,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 2.1386.7.}}

4490:
Included with: ** ῥητ-ός**, ή, όν,
(ἐρῶ) **stated, specified, covenanted**, μισθῷ ἔπι ῥ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.445}}; παρεῖναι ἐς χρόνον ῥ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.77, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.124}}; ἐν ἡμέραις ῥ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.29; ἐπὶ ῥητοῖς γέρασι πατρικαὶ βασιλεῖαι [prev. author] 1.13}}; ῥ. ἀργύριον a **stated** sum, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.7, 4.69}}; ἐπὶ ῥητοῖσι, _Attic dialect_ ἐπὶ ῥητοῖς, on **stated terms**, on **certain conditions**, according to **covenant**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.57, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 459, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.122, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.22, +others; παρέσεσθαι εἰς ῥ. ἡμέραν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.6}}; ῥ. ἀπόκρισις a **distinct, definite** answer, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.6.7: ῥητόν, τό}}, **fixed date** for a lawsuit, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.463.14 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}; so perhaps ἀπὸ ῥητῶν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 12 (9).1273 (Euboea, 6th c.BC).}} _adverb_ -τῶς **expressly, distinctly**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.23.5, “SIG” 685.77 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.105 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, NT.1Tim.4.1, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).427}}: _superlative_ ῥητότατα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.16.}}
**2** **spoken of, famous**, ῥητοί τ᾽ ἄρρητοί τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 4.}}
**3** of language, **in common use** (= συνήθης), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “Pron.” 113.18; φράσις [prev. author] “Synt.” 39.15}}. _adverb_ **-τῶς**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.161 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**II** **that may be spoken** or **told**, εἰ ῥητόν, φράσον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 765}}; ἦ ῥητό; ἢ οὐχὶ θεμιτὸν ἄλλον εἰδένα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 993}}; αὐδῶν ἀνόσι᾽ οὐδὲ ῥητά μοι{{Refs|[prev. work] 1289; ῥ. ἄρρητόν τ᾽ ἔπος [prev. author] “OC” 1001}}; δεινὸν γάρ, οὐδὲ ῥ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 756; compare ἄρρητος 111.3.}}
**II.2** **that can be spoken** or **enunciated**, συλλαβή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 202b, compare 205d, 205e; διάλεκτοι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.110S.; οὐ ῥ. κατ᾽ ἰδίαν αἱ ἐγκλιτικαί 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 36.30}}; **communicable in words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 341c.}}
**III** in Mathematics texts, **rational**, of magnitudes, opposed to surds (ἄλογα), ῥητὰ πρὸς ἄλληλα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 546c, “Hp.Ma.” 303b, compare 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 10 “Deff.” 3 and 4, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus}} “*Deff.” 128; in Metric, ῥ. πούς, opposed to ἄλογος, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Aristides Quintilianus Musicus 1.14}}; see at {ἄρρητος} IV.
**IV** τὸ ῥ. **the precise, literal contents** of a document, **the letter** of the law, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 2.36, etc.}}; ῥητός **literal**, opposed to allegorical, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.69, +others}}
**V** ={ῥῆμα} {{Refs|I.3, even of}}**a living thing**, Hebrew **dâvâr**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.9.4.}}

4491:
** ῥίζ-α**, ης, ἡ:
_Ionic dialect_ _nominative_ ῥίζη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus cited in 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus, _accusative_ ῥίζην 2nd c.AD: Marcellus Sidetes Poeta Medicus 89 (before a vowel), but ῥίζαν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.846 (whence _Ionic dialect_ _nominative_ ῥίζα may be inferred)}}:—**root**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.304, 23.196, etc.}}; used as a medicine, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.846}}; ῥ. ἐλατήριος, of a purgative medicine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.34}}: mostly in _plural_, **roots**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.134, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.435, etc.; δένδρεα μακρὰ αὐτῇσιν ῥίζησι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.542: hence}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **roots of the eye**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.390 (but ῥίζας ἐν ὄσσοις αἱματῶπας in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 933}} probably bloodshot **streaks**); the **roots** or **foundations** of the earth, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 19; χθόνα.. αὐταῖς ῥ. πνεῦμα κραδαίνοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1047 (_anapaest meter_); ἰπούμενος ῥίζαισιν Αἰτναίαις ὕπο [prev. work] 367}}; of feathers, hair, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 251b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 518b14}}; of the teeth, {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 789a13; γαστρὸς ῥ. ὀμφαλός [prev. author] “HA” 493a18, etc.}}
**3** τὸν πόλεμον ἐκ ῥιζῶν ἀνῄρηκε 'root and branch', {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 21, compare 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus Pont. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.523f; ἐκ ῥιζῶν ἀπώλεσεν LXX.Job.31.12}}; compare ῥιζόθεν, πρόρριζος.
**II** **that from which anything springs as from a root**, ῥίζαν ἀπείρου τρίταν a third continental **foundation**, of Libya, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.8}}; ἀστέων ῥ., of Cyrene, as the **root** or **original** of the Cyrenaic Pentapolis, {{Refs|[prev. work] 4.15}}; **root** or **stock** from which a family springs, ῥ. σπέρματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “O.” 2.46, compare “I.” 8(7).61, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 966, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1178, etc.}}; so, **race, family**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 755 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 610, “OGI” 383.31 (Nemrud Dagh, 2nd c.BC), etc.}}; συκοφάντου.. σπέρμα καὶ ῥ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.48}}; **sect, party**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “contra Galilaeos” 106e; also ῥ. κακῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 912.11 (_anapaest meter_); ἀρχὴ καὶ ῥ. παντὸς ἀγαθοῦ 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 409, cf. NT.1Tim.6.10; πηγὴ καὶ ῥ. καλοκἀγαθίας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.4c; ἀρχαὶ καὶ ῥ. γῆς καὶ θαλάττης 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 353b1, etc.; compare ῥίζωμα 11.}}
**II.2** **base, foundation**, ῥ. πάντων καὶ βάσις ἁ γᾶ ἐρήρεισται Ti. _Locrian dialect_{{Refs 5th c.BC+|97e,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 81c}}; **base** of a vertical pillar, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 3.23; τῶν λόφων 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 10.6.}}
**II.3** in Mathematics texts, **root** or **base** of a series, Anatolius cited in {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 9}}. (_Aeolic dialect_ **βρίζα** (which see): cf. Gothic _waúrts_, Latin _radix._)

4492:
** ῥιζ-όω**,
**cause to strike root**: _metaphorically_, **plant, fix firmly**, ὅς μιν [τὴν ναῦν] λᾶαν θῆκε καὶ ἐρρίζωσεν ἔνερθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.163}}; [νήσους] κατὰ βυσσὸν πρυμνόθεν (assuming variant) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 35}}:—_passive_, of trees and plants, **take root, strike root**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.9, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.2.1:}} —_middle_, ἄριστον ῥιζώσασθαι, of the fig, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HP” 2.5.6}}; so αἱ πίνναι ἐρρίζωνται, opposed to ἀρρίζωτοι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 548a5; ῥ. ἐπί τινος “Anthologia Graeca” 6.66 (6th c.AD: Paulus Silentiarius Epigrammaticus)}}; ὀδὸς βάθροισι γῆθεν ἐρριζωμένος **made fast** or **solid**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1591}}; of a bridge, αἰώνιος ἐρρίζωται {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 1078.7 (from Adana).}}
**2** _metaphorically_, ἐρρίζωσε τὴν τυραννίδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64:}} —_passive_, τυραννὶς ἐρριζωμένη{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 60, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 839a}}; ἐξ ἀμαθίας πάντα κακὰ ἐρρ. **have their root** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ep.” 336b, cf. S 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1.271}}; ἐν ἀγάπῃ ἐρρ. {{NT|NT.Eph.3.18.}}
**II** _passive_ also of land, **to be planted with trees**, ἀλωὴ ἐρρίζωται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.122.}}

4493:
** ῥῑπ-ή**, ἡ,
(ῥίπτω) poet. Noun, **swing** or **force with which anything is thrown**, ὅσση δ᾽ αἰγανέης ῥιπὴ.. τέτυκται as far as is the **flight** of a javelin, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.589; λᾶος ὑπὸ ῥιπῆς 12.462, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.192}}; πέτριναι ῥ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1123 (Lyric poetry)}}; βελέων ῥ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.68}}; ὑπὸ ῥιπῆς.. Βορέαο **the sweep** or **rush** of the N. wind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.171, 19.358, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.46; κυμάτων ῥιπαὶ ἀνέμων τε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.195, compare “Parth.” 2.20, “fragment” 88.2; ῥ. ἀνέμων [prev. author] “P.” 9.48, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 137}} (Lyric poetry, here _metaphorically_ of **gusts** of passion, compare {{Refs|930)}}; ῥ. Διόθεν τεύχουσα φόβον **storm**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1089 (_anapaest meter_), compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1016}}; ῥ. πυρός **rush** of fire, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.12; ἀνδρός 8.355; ἀθανάτων 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 681, 849}}; κεραυνῶν, χαλάζης, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 3.21, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 14.77}}; ὑπὸ ῥιπῇς Ἀφροδίτης, of love, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 4.141}}; νυχιᾶν (ἐννυχιᾶν Lachm.) ἀπὸ ῥιπᾶν from the night **storms**, i.e. from the “North”, the land of darkness and storms, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1248 (Lyric poetry, but Scholia understands Ῥιπᾶν, see at {Ῥῖπαι}).}}]
**2** πτερύγων ῥιπαί **flapping** of wings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 126 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 594.4}}; **buzz** of a gnat's wing, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 893}}; of the lyre's **quivering notes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.10.}}
**3** **quivering, twinkling light**, ῥιπαὶ ἄστρων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 106 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**3.b** of any **rapid movement**, ῥ. ποδῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 885 (Lyric poetry)}}; ῥ. ὠκυάλῳ, of a dolphin, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 2.535}}; of a bird's wing, οὐδὲ τινάσσει ῥιπήν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.935}}; ἐν ῥ. ὀφθαλμοῦ the **twinkling** of an eye, {{NT|NT.1Cor.}}
**4** **a strong smell**, ῥ. οἴνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 166.}}

4494:
** ῥῑπ-ίζω**,
(ῥιπίς) **blow up** or **fan** the flame, πολέμου ἔριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Cypria “fragment” 1; στάσιν ἀνεγείρει καὶ ῥιπίζει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 360; ῥ. πῦρ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 21; φλόγα “Anthologia Graeca” 5.121 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Diodorus Epigrammaticus):}} —_passive_, τεμάχη ῥιπίζεται the fish **is fanned to boiling-point**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 842.}}
**2** **fan** a person, in _middle_, **fan oneself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 16}}:—_passive_, ῥιπίζεσθαι ὑπὸ τῶν περιστερῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 202.5}}; **to be fanned** or **blown about**, ὑπ᾽ ἀνέμου {{NT+4th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 1324, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 967a21; πρὸς ἀνέμων 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.511; κλύδων ἀνεμιζόμενος καὶ ῥιπιζόμενος NT.Jam.1.6; ῥιπιζομένη ἄχνη 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 70.4: _metaphorically_, ῥ. ταῖς ἐλπίσι 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.47.}}
**3** **hurl**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.32, compare 30 (_passive_).}}

4495:
** ῥίπτ-ω**,
also **ῥιπτέω**, and (in _frequentative_ sense) **ῥιπτάζω** (which see): —_Ionic dialect_ Iterat. ῥίπτασκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.23, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.592}}, **-εσκον** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 26}}: _future_ ῥίψω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.13, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἔρριψα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|23.842, etc. (ἔριψα 6th c.BC: Arion Lyricus 18, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 3.32, ἀπέριψα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.37)}}, _Epic dialect_ ῥῖψα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.378}}; also _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _aorist 2_ ἔρρῐφε {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.350}}: _perfect_ ἔρριφα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.9}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ῥίψαντο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 6.10, ἀπο-ρίψασθαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.146:}} —_passive_, _future_ ῥιφθήσομαι (ἀπορ-) {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1019; ῥῐφήσομαι LXX.Eze.7.19, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 3 (variant in S.[same place]); 3}} _future_ ἐρρίψομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 17}}: _aorist_ ἐρρίφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 484 (ἀπο-), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 10 (variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 944d}}; also ἐρρίφην [ῐ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 335, “fragment” 489, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 944a, Sositheus Tragicus 3, etc.; poetry ἐρίφην “Anthologia Graeca” 12.234 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus):}}_perfect_ ἔρριμμαι Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1404 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 850, etc.; poetry reduplicate ῥερίφθαι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 318, compare “PMag.Par.” 1.194, 2039}} (ἀπο-): _pluperfect_ ἔρριπτο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 17. [ῑ by nature, Hdn.Gr. 2.10}}; frequently written with ει in later Inscrr. (compare ῥιπτέω, καταρρίπτω) and Papyri, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.38 W., (προσ-) “Rh.” 2.94 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; the _Epic dialect_ _aorist 1_ is ῥῖψα, not ῥίψα: ῐ in _future_ 2 and _aorist 2_ _passive_]:—**throw, cast, hurl**, σόλον, σφαῖραν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.842, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.115; χερσί 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.57; ῥ. ἀπὸ βηλοῦ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.591, etc.; ἤ μιν ἑλὼν ῥίψω ἐς Τάρταρον 8.13, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1051 (_anapaest meter_); ἐς τὸ δυστυχές [prev. author] “Ch.” 913; ἐς φλόγα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 695; ποτὶ νέφεα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.592}}; χθονὶ ῥ. ἑαυτόν **throw** on the ground, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 790, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 39 (_anapaest meter_); ἐς ὕδωρ ψυχρόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49: _absolutely_, ἐρριμμένος}} **prostrate**, ἐρριμμένους καὶ μεθύοντας {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.48.2}}; ἔτι τῶν νεκρῶν.. ἐρριμμένων ἐπὶ τῆς ἀγορᾶς **lying**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 28; κλῶνας ἔχουσα ἐπὶ γῆς ἐρριμμένους 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.29}}, compare 4.169; ἔρριπται νεκροῖς ὅμοια, of hibernating animals, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.67; τὰ μελίσσεια ἐν ἀγρῷ ἔριπται}}**have been deposited,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 467.5 (3rd c.BC)}}; **cast** a net, ἔρριπται ὁ βόλος the cast **have been made**, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62}}; αὐτοῦ χερμάδας.. ἔρριπτον **threw** stones **at** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1097, compare “Cyc.” 51 (Lyric poetry)}}; ῥ. τινὰ πρὸς πέτραν **throw** him against a rock, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 780}}; but κατὰ στύφλου πέτρας down from a rock, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1430, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 748}}; κατὰ κρημνῶν down a precipice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 944a (_passive_)}}; ὠλένας πρὸς οὐρανόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1096.}}
**II** like{ῥιπτάζω}, ῥ. ἑωυτήν **toss** oneself **about**, as in a fever, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.2; ἐπὶ λαιὰ καὶ ἐπὶ δεξιὰ σαυτόν “Anthologia Graeca” 5.118 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus)}}: generally, **throw about**, πλοκάμους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 758, compare “Ba.” 150 (both Lyric poetry)}}; **winnow**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.541.}}
**III** **cast out** of house or land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 719, “Ph.” 265, etc.; μὴ ῥιφθῶ κυσὶν πρόβλητος [prev. author] “Aj.” 830.}}
**IV** **throw off** or **away**, of arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 820}}; of clothes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474a, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.12}}; so ἔρριψε Πάγασος δεσπόταν **threw** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).44}}; especially ῥ. ἀσπίδα (compare ῥίψασπις), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 10.9, etc.; βιβλίον “PUniv.Giss.” 20.12 (2nd c.AD).}}
**V** ῥ. λόγους **cast** them **forth, hurl** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 314, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 680; τὸ προειρημένον ἀναποδείκτως ἐρρίφθαι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.57 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; also, **throw** them **away, waste** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1068, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1404 (_anapaest meter_, _passive_)}}; λόγοι μάτην ῥιφέντες {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hec.” 335}}; so οἴχεται.. ταῦτ᾽ ἐρριμμένα **set at naught**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1271.}}
**VI** ῥ. ἐπὶ πάντας τοὺς κλήρους, as in a scramble, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 617e; ῥ. πάντα κύβον κεφαλῆς ὕπερθεν ἐμῆς “Anthologia Graeca” 5.24 ([1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus]): hence ῥ. κίνδυνον}} **make a bold throw, run a risk**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 402.7.}}
**VII** ῥ. ἑαυτόν **throw** or **cast** oneself **down**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.25}}: _absolutely_, **fling oneself**, ἐς πόντον {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 176; ἐς ἅλμην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 166; τάφρον ἐς κοίλην [prev. author] Alcaeus 897 (_anapaest meter_); ῥ. ἐν πένθει κατὰ δρία [prev. author] “Hel.” 1325 (Lyric poetry), compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 312, Vett. Val. 126.22; compare βάλλω 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 111.}}
**VIII** uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 264.}}

